The comparison in Table~\ref{tab:intro-comparison} concerns estimation of a specified output probability. \cite{BGL} additionally give a quasipolynomial-time approximate-sampling algorithm and, in their general construction, an approximation to the output state, under the peakedness assumption $\max_x P(x)\ge n^{-a}$ for a fixed constant $a$. We emphasize that
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} gives polynomial-time probability
estimation without any peakedness assumption, but does not
provide polynomial-time algorithms for those stronger tasks.
In particular, separate additive estimates of individual probabilities need not form a normalized distribution or guarantee bounded total variation error.
Extending our work to polynomial-time sampling
of arbitrary-connectivity peaked shallow circuits is a natural open question.

\subsection{Proof Strategy}

Suppose the target output bitstring is $x \in \{0,1\}^n$. Since this is a shallow circuit, we can brute-force compute the probability that any single output bit agrees with the corresponding bit in $x$. Consider the set of \textit{bad} output bits $\mathcal B$ whose probability of agreeing with their corresponding bit in $x$ is less than some threshold $1-\tau$. The starting point of \cite{BGL} (and prior work on this matter) is to observe that in shallow quantum circuits, the probability of measuring the outcome $x$ is bounded by $(1-\tau)^{\Theta(|\mathcal B|)}$, and so $|\mathcal B|$ must be small if $x$ is to be measured with non-negligible probability. On the remaining set of \textit{good} output bits, the output distribution is heavily concentrated around bitstrings that mostly agree with $x$, with deviations bounded by $\tau$. It is known how to estimate marginals of such distributions to relative-error, via a cluster expansion that converges when $\tau$ is a sufficiently low constant~\cite{mann2025approximate,Barvinok}. Our main contribution is to show that the output probability of $x$ can be expressed as a sum of marginals on the \textit{good} bits, weighted by marginals on regions that connect the \textit{good} and \textit{bad} bits. In particular, we show that enumerating and evaluating this expression using only small connecting regions produces the desired additive error estimate.

\input{lightcones}

To provide intuition for this decomposition, we first construct a connectivity graph $G$ by drawing edges between bits that could be correlated with each other.  In shallow circuits, this graph has bounded degree, because two output bits need to share a ``light cone'' in the circuit in order to be correlated. We will apply an inclusion-exclusion argument and use these lightcone properties to write $|\langle x|U|0^n\rangle|^2$ in the following form:
\begin{align}
    &\Pr[\text{all bits agree with $x$}] \notag \\
    &=\notag
\sum_{\substack{T \subseteq [n] \backslash \mathcal B\text{: all connected components of $G[T]$ touch $\mathcal B$}}}

(-1)^{|T|} \\
&\times \Pr[\text{all bits in $\mathcal{B}$ agree with $x$, \textcolor{figAttach}{all bits in $T$ disagree with $x$}}] \label{eq:bad}\\
&\times \Pr[\text{all bits outside the neighborhood of $T \cup \mathcal B$ agree with $x$}] \label{eq:good}
\end{align}

 Line (\ref{eq:bad}) isolates how the positive influence of the \textit{good} bits extends into the \textit{bad} bits: because \textit{good} bits should be unlikely to disagree with $x$ (as we have highlighted in purple), this term decays roughly exponentially with the number of such good bits, i.e. $|T|$. For sufficiently small $\tau$, this decay dominates the number of possible choices of $T $. Thus, we can enumerate only the terms corresponding to small-sized $T $, truncate the remaining terms, and obtain the desired additive error. In the resulting approximation, the term in line (\ref{eq:bad}) can be computed by brute force because the aforementioned argument of Bravyi-Gosset-Liu \cite{BGL} guarantees that $\mathcal B$ is also small (so the full set $T \cup \mathcal B$ is small). The term in line (\ref{eq:good}) can be computed using the aforementioned cluster expansion \cite{mann2025approximate,Barvinok} because it is a marginal on only \textit{good} bits.

 \input{good-bad-decomposition}

\subsection{Implications for expectation values}
\label{sec:intro-expectations}
Theorem~\ref{thm:main} also gives polynomial-time additive
estimation of the magnitude of product-operator expectation
values. For single-qubit operators $A_i$ with
$\|A_i\|\le1$, let
\[
 m=\langle0^n|U^\dagger
       \left(\bigotimes_{i=1}^n A_i\right)
       U|0^n\rangle.
\]
The construction used in~\cite{BGM,BGL} expresses $|m|^2$ as an output
probability of a circuit on at most $2n$ qubits with depth
at most $2d+1$, where $d$ is the depth of $U$.
Estimating this probability to additive error
$\varepsilon^2$, projecting the estimate onto $[0,1]$,
and taking its square root therefore estimates $|m|$
to additive error $\varepsilon$ in
$\poly(n,1/\varepsilon)$ time for fixed depth and gate arity.

When $m$ is known to be nonnegative, this procedure also
estimates $m$ itself. For a general real expectation value,
however, the squared magnitude does not determine its sign. Similarly,
for a complex expectation value, it does not determine its
phase. Thus the task of polynomial-time estimation of general signed or complex expectation values remains an open problem.

\section{Setup and preliminaries}\label{sec:model}
Throughout, $\log$ denotes the natural logarithm and $\log_2$ the base-two logarithm. Denote the set $\{1, \ldots, n\}$ for $n \in \mathbb{N}$ as $[n]$. Our computational model includes all $n$-qubit, depth-$d$, and $k$-local quantum circuits where the gates at each of the $d$ layers of the circuit act on disjoint sets of at most $k$ qubits. Following the brute-force-style calculations in~\cite{BGM}, we state running times for computing output probabilities exactly. Thus, the approximation error comes from truncating inclusion-exclusion and the subroutine $\mathsf{Good}$ defined in Section~\ref{sec:algorithm}.

Following the notation in $\cite{BGL}$, we are interested in estimating the output probability
\[P(x) = |\langle x | U |0^n \rangle|^2,\]
where $U$ is an $n$-qubit, depth-$d$, and $k$-local quantum circuit and $x$ is a length-$n$ variable bitstring.

For the purpose of our work, it suffices to construct a classical algorithm to estimate $P(0^n) = |\langle 0^n | U | 0^n \rangle|^2$.
For any computational basis input or output strings $x, y$,  we can simply conjugate $U$ with the appropriate bit-flip operators to give us $|\langle y | U | x \rangle|^2 = |\langle 0^n | X^y U X^x | 0^n \rangle|^2$. For $S \subseteq [n]$, define $x_S$ to be the restriction of $x$ to the indices in $S$. Further define the marginal $P_S(x_S)$ to be the probability of measuring $x_S$ on all qubits in $S$, and abbreviate to $P(x_S)$ when it is clear which qubits are involved.
Throughout, we will use the notation $0_S$ and $1_S$ to denote the measurement outcome bitstring of all $0$'s or all $1$'s, respectively, on the qubits in $S$.
When $S$ is the singleton set $\{i\}$, we abbreviate the measurement outcome as $0_i$ and $1_i$.

Finally, let $\widehat{P}$ denote the probability estimator and use the same notation convention for the marginal probabilities.
For a specified outcome $x_S$, let $\widehat P(x_S)$ denote an estimate of $P(x_S)$. Note that estimates for different outcomes or subsets need not be marginals of a common probability distribution.

Define the backward light cone of an output qubit $i$ as the set of input qubits with a directed path through the gates in the circuit to output qubit $i$. Let $\mathcal{L}(i)$ be this set. Similarly, we abuse the notation slightly and define
\[\mathcal{L}(A) \equiv \bigcup_{j \in A} \mathcal{L}(j),\qquad A \subseteq [n].\]
If two subsets of qubits $A, B \subseteq [n]$ satisfy the property that $\mathcal{L}(A) \cap \mathcal{L}(B) = \emptyset$, then we say that $A$ and $B$ are \emph{lightcone-separated}.
We construct a \emph{light-cone overlap graph} $G = (V, E)$ where $|V| = n$. We assign one vertex per output qubit and introduce an edge $(i, j) \in E$ if and only if $\mathcal{L}(i) \cap \mathcal{L}_(j) \neq \emptyset$ for $i \neq j$. Denote the maximum degree of $G$ as $K$. A simple light-cone bound gives us $K \leq k^{2d}$. We denote closed neighborhoods of a subset of vertices $S \subseteq V$ as $N_G[S]$ where $S \subseteq N_G[S]$.

We present a set of well-known light-cone properties in the following lemma.

\begin{lemma}[Basic light-cone facts]\label{lemma:light-cone-facts}
    The graph $G$ can be constructed in polynomial time for fixed constants $d$ and $k$. The marginal has the following properties:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $0\le P(x_S)\le P(x_T)\le1$ whenever $T\subseteq S$.
        \item If no edge joins disjoint sets $S,T$, their full measurement output vectors are independent. In particular,
        \[
         P(x_{S\cup T})=P(x_S)P(x_T).
        \]
    \end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
    Because each layer of the backward light cone increases the number of relevant qubits by at most a factor of $k$, the size of a backward light cone for any output qubit $i$ is given by $|\mathcal{L}(i)| \leq k^d$. Similarly, each of the input qubits in $\mathcal{L}(i)$ can reach a set of at most $k^d$ output qubits using the same argument. Therefore, every output qubit has a backward light cone that has nontrivial overlap with the backward light cone at most $k^{2d}$ other output qubits, giving us $K \leq k^{2d}$ which is a constant. Enumerating these bounded-size cones takes constant time to construct the edges for each of the $n$ vertices so the graph can be constructed in $O_{d, k}(nk^{2d})$ operations with polynomial bit overhread for labels.
    For (1), the monotocity shown in the expression is immediate from the event interpretations of $P(x_S)$ and $P(x_T)$.
    For (2), the absence of an edge joining sets $S$ and $T$ implies that the backpropagated measurement operators on $S$ and $T$ necessarily act on disjoint input qubits. Since the input qubits are assumed to be in a product state, the joint expectation with respect to the measurement operators will factor. This proves the independence of the two entire collections.
\end{proof}

\section{Isolating the bad bits}\label{sec:core}
First, we will choose some threshold $0\leq\tau\leq 1 $ and define $\mathcal{B}$ and $\mathcal{G}$ as the set of bad and good bits respectively,
\[\mathcal{B} = \left\{i\,:\,P(1_i) > \tau \right\},\qquad \mathcal{G} = [n] \setminus \mathcal{B}\]
For reasons that will be apparent in Section \ref{sec:small}, we will fix 

\begin{align}
    \tau =
 \left(\frac{1}{e^{2}(2K+1)}\right)^{K+1}
\end{align}

First, we have the following bound on the size of $\mathcal{B}$

\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:when_to_output_zero}
    If $\sum_{i \in [n]} P(1_{i}) \geq (K+1)\log\frac{1}{\varepsilon}$, then $P(0^n) \leq\varepsilon$. Otherwise,
    \begin{align}
        |\mathcal B| \leq (K+1)\left(\frac{1}{e^{2}(2K+1)}\right)^{-(K+1)} \log\frac{1}{\varepsilon}
    \end{align}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Color the vertices of the graph $G$ with $K + 1$ colors. By the pigeonhole principle, we can find at least one color $C$ such that for all output qubits $i \in C$, we have $\sum_{i \in C} P(1_i) \geq  \sum_{i \in [n]} P(1_{i}) / (K + 1)$. The output qubits in $C$ are mutually independent, i.e., they are light-cone separated, and therefore,
    \begin{align*}
        P(0^n) &\leq \prod_{i \in C} (1 - P(1_i)) \leq e^{-\sum_{i \in C}P(1_i)} \leq e^{-\sum_{i \in [n]} P(1_i)/(K+1)}
    \end{align*}
    Thus, when $\sum_{i \in [n]} P(1_{i}) \geq (K+1)\log\frac{1}{\varepsilon}$, we have the preliminary condition. Otherwise, observe that each bit in $\mathcal{B}$ contributes $\tau$ to $\sum_{i \in [n]} P(1_{i})$, i.e. 
    \begin{align}
        |\mathcal{B}|\tau \leq \sum_{i \in [n]} P(1_{i}) \leq (K+1)\log \frac{1}{\varepsilon}
    \end{align}
    which obtains the main bound.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Separating the contributions of good and bad bits}
The core insight of our work is to pull apart the joint probability $P(0^n)$ into marginal probabilities of good bits and marginal probabilities of bad bits with a limited number of attached good bits.

An inclusion-exclusion decomposition expresses $P(0^n)$ in terms of mixed outcomes that are zero on the bad bits and one on selected good bits:
\begin{align}
    P(0^n) =\sum_{S \subseteq [n] \backslash B }(-1)^{|S|}P(0_{\mathcal B},1_S)
\end{align}
Our starting point is to observe that many of the terms in the sum decompose into a product of probabilities due to the shallowness of the circuit. Recall from Section~\ref{sec:model} that we can construct a light-cone overlap graph $G$ that has constant degree by assigning edges between vertices that correspond to bits that are correlated with each other. Now, we will write each $S$ as $S = T \sqcup S \backslash T$ where $T$ contains all the bits of $S$ that form connected components adjacenet to $\mathcal B$ . Further, for any $T$, let $T' =[n]\setminus N_G[\mathcal B\cup T] \subseteq [n]\backslash \mathcal B$ denote all bits that are not adjacent to $T$ or $\mathcal B$.
\begin{align}
    P(0^n) &=    \sum_{S \subseteq [n] \backslash \mathcal B }(-1)^{|S|}P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T) P(1_{S\setminus T})\\
    &=    \sum_{T \text{ consists of conn. comp. touching } \mathcal B}(-1)^{|T|}P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)\left[\sum_{S \subseteq T'}(-1)^{|S|}P(1_S)\right]\\
    &=    \sum_{T \text{ consists of conn. comp. touching } \mathcal B}(-1)^{|T|}P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)\left[P(0_{T'}) \right]\\
\end{align}

However, this is still not a tractable decomposition to evaluate since $T$ can be very large. We next prove that we can safely truncate the sum to only include small sets $T$.



\subsection{Bounding the bad bits with few good bits connected to them}
Notice that by definition, good bits have a small marginal probability of disagreeing with target string $0^n$. In fact, the joint probability decays exponentially as the number of good bits involved increases. This means that the terms associated with sets $T$ that have many good bits have very small contribution, and can be safely truncated as we show next.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:truncating_error}
    \begin{equation}
        |P(0^n)-\widehat{P}(0^n)| \leq \varepsilon ,
    \end{equation}
    where
    \begin{equation}
        \widehat{P}(0^n) =\sum_{\substack{T \text{ path-connected to } \mathcal B\\|T| \leq \ell}}(-1)^{|T|}P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)\left[P(0_{T'}) \right]\\ \label{eq:good-bad-decomp}
    \end{equation}
    and
    \begin{equation}
        \ell = |\mathcal B|  + \left\lceil\log_2 \frac{1}{\varepsilon}\right\rceil
    \end{equation}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Our goal is to bound, 
    \begin{align}
        |P(0^n)-\widehat{P}(0^n)| &= \left|
\sum_{\substack{T \text{ consists of conn. comp. touching } \mathcal B\\ |T| > \ell}}
(-1)^{|T|}
P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)
\left[P(0_{T'})\right]
\right| \\
& \leq \sum_{\substack{T \text{ consists of conn. comp. touching } \mathcal B\\ |T| > \ell}}
P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)
    \end{align}
    Consider any $T$ of size $|T| = s$. Note that by choosing $s/(K+1)$ qubits in $T$ with non-overlapping lightcones, we have
    \begin{align}
        P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T) \leq \tau^{s/(K+1)}
    \end{align}
    Note the exponential decay in $s$. To finish the bound, we must bound the number of valid choices of $T$ of size $s$. Fix an ordering $b_1,\ldots,b_{|\mathcal B|}$ of the vertices in $\mathcal B$. For each valid $T$, assign each connected component of $G[T]$ to the first vertex in this ordering adjacent to that component. Such a vertex exists because $T$ is path-connected to $\mathcal B$.

Let $s_i$ be the total number of vertices in the components assigned to $b_i$. Then $s_1+\cdots+s_{|\mathcal B|}=s$, with each $s_i\geq 0$. For $|\mathcal B|\geq 1$, there are
$\binom{s+|\mathcal B|-1}{|\mathcal B|-1}\leq 2^{|\mathcal B|+s}$
possible choices of these sizes. The union of $b_i$ and all components assigned to it is a connected set of size $s_i+1$ containing $b_i$, so there are at most $(eK)^{s_i}$ choices for this union. These unions determine $T$, and hence
\begin{align}
    \left|\{T:T \text{ path-connected to } \mathcal B,\ |T|=s\}\right|
    \leq 2^{|\mathcal B|+s}\prod_{i=1}^{|\mathcal B|}(eK)^{s_i}
    = 2^{|\mathcal B|+s}(eK)^s.
\end{align}






    Putting this together:
    \begin{align}
    \sum_{\substack{T \text{ path-connected to } \mathcal B\\ |T| > \ell}}
    P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)
    &\leq \sum_{s=\ell+1}^{\infty}
    \sum_{\substack{T \text{ path-connected to } \mathcal B\\ |T| = s}}
    P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T) \\
    &\leq 2^{|\mathcal B|} \sum_{s=\ell+1}^{\infty}
    \left(2eK\tau^{1/(K+1)}\right)^s \\
    &= 2^{|\mathcal B|} \sum_{s=\ell+1}^{\infty}
    \left(\frac{2K}{e(2K+1)}\right)^s \\
    &< 2^{|\mathcal B|} \sum_{s=\ell+1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{2^s} \\
    &= 2^{|\mathcal B|-\ell} \\
    &\leq \varepsilon,
\end{align}
    where in the second equality we plugged in $\tau = \left(\frac{1}{e^2(2K+1)}\right)^{K+1}$ and in the last inequality we used $\ell = |\mathcal B|+\left\lceil\log_2\frac{1}{\varepsilon}\right\rceil$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Computing the marginal probability of the good bits using cluster expansion}\label{sec:small}
It remains to compute the marginals $P(0_W)$ for $W \subseteq \mathcal{G}$. Since each qubit in $\mathcal{G}$ is ``good'', they each have a low marginal probability of disagreeing with the target bit. Furthermore, by the light-cone structure of the circuit, each output bit only depends on a limited number of other output bits. It turns out that joint probabilities with this structure can be efficiently  estimated to small relative error using cluster expansion \cite{mann2025approximate,Barvinok}. In fact, we can apply the following theorem directly by letting $E_v$ be the event that the output of qubit $v$ agrees with the target bit.
\begin{theorem}[Mann--Waite, Theorem 2 of~\cite{mann2025approximate}]\label{thm:MW}\label{thm:MM}
Fix $K \in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge2}$. Let $\{E_v\}_{v\in V(G)}$ be a set of events in a probability space with strong dependency graph $G$ of maximum degree $K$. Suppose that, for all $v\in V(G)$,
\[
 \Pr\!\left[\overline{E_v}\right]\le
 \left(\frac{1}{e^{2}(2K+1)}\right)^{K+1}.
\]
If for all $S\subseteq V(G)$, $\Pr\left[\bigcap_{v\in S}\overline{E_v}\right]$ can be computed in time $\exp(O(|S|))$, then there is a fully polynomial-time approximation scheme for estimating probability of $\Pr\left[\bigcap_{v\in V(G)}E_v \right]$ that has relative error $\varepsilon$ and runtime $\text{poly}(|V(G)|,\varepsilon^{-1})$.
\end{theorem}

 \begin{corollary} \label{corr:MW}
     When $K \geq 2$, for any set of good bits, $T' \subseteq \mathcal{G}$, there exists a deterministic classical algorithm that estimates $P(0_{T'}) $ to multiplicative error $\varepsilon$ in runtime $\poly(|T'|,\varepsilon^{-1})$.
 \end{corollary}
 \begin{proof}
     For our all-zero target, $\Pr[\overline{E_v}]=P(1_v)$, $\Pr[\bigcap_{v\in S}\overline{E_v}]=P(1_S)$, $\Pr[\bigcap_{v\in W}E_v]=P(0_W)$, and $P(1_v) \le \tau =  \left(\frac{1}{e^{2}(2K+1)}\right)^{K+1}$. 
 \end{proof}

We can handle the $K=0$ and $K=1$ cases trivially.

\section{Algorithm design}\label{sec:algorithm}

We combine the inclusion-exclusion decomposition detailed in Section~\ref{sec:core} with the
Mann--Waite result of Section~\ref{sec:small} for our classical algorithm which we state in the following theorem.

\begin{theorem}[Main theorem]
\label{thm:algorithm-main}
Fix positive integers $d,k$. Let $U$ be an $n$-qubit unitary
quantum circuit with $d$ layers, each consisting of gates on disjoint sets
of at most $k$ qubits. Let $L_{\rm in}$ be the length of its classical
description.
Given $x\in\{0,1\}^n$ and $0<\varepsilon<1/2$, there is a
deterministic classical algorithm using $\poly_{d,k}(n,L_{\rm in},1/\varepsilon)$ operations returning
$\widehat P(x)\in[0,1]$ such that
\[
 \left|\widehat P(x)-|\langle x|U|0^n\rangle|^2\right|
 \le\varepsilon.
\]
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Definitions and subroutines}
We reduce an arbitrary target string to $0^n$ by appending the appropriate single-qubit bit flips. This adds at most one layer and does not enlarge the backward light cones, so their size bound still uses the original depth $d$. We use the following two subroutines. The subroutine $\mathsf{Brute}(S,z_S)$ returns $P(z_S)$ exactly. After a polynomial-time construction of the backward light cone corresponding to the set $S$ of output bits with $|S| \geq 1$, we can evaluate $P(z_S)$ exactly with time
\begin{equation}\label{eq:brute-runtime}
 T_{\mathsf{Brute}}(S)
 =O\!\left(dk^d |S|\,2^{k^d |S|+k}\right)
 =2^{O_{d,k}(|S|)}.
\end{equation}
The extra bit flips we append do not change the asymptotic bound. This is a $k$-local generalization of \cite{BGM}'s exact calculation in time $2^{O(2^d s)}$ for two-qubit gates. We emphasize that $\mathsf{Brute}$ returns the exact probability value.

For $W\subseteq\mathcal G$ and $0<\eta<1$, the subroutine $\mathsf{Good}(W,\eta)$ returns an estimate in $[0,1]$ of $P(0_W)$ with additive error at most $\eta$, in $\poly_{d,k}(n,L_{\rm in},1/\eta)$ operations. The subroutine essentially applies Theorem~\ref{thm:MW} with relative error $\eta$ and evaluates its marginals $P(1_S)$ exactly by $\mathsf{Brute}$. Since $P(0_W)\le 1$, a relative error of at most $\eta$ implies an additive error of at most $\eta$. Note that projecting the resulting estimate onto $[0,1]$ cannot increase its error. Both subroutines are also defined to return one on the empty set.

\subsection{Algorithm}
We construct Algorithm~\ref{alg:main} for the classical algorithm stated in Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm-main}.
\begin{algorithm}[H]
\caption{Additive estimation of a specified output probability}
\label{alg:main}
\small
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require A circuit $U$, a target string $x\in\{0,1\}^n$,
         and $0<\varepsilon<1/2$.
\Ensure A $\widehat P(x)\in[0,1]$ satisfying
        $|\widehat P(x)-P(x)|\le\varepsilon$.

\State $U \gets \left(\prod_{i=1}^n X_i^{x_i}\right)U$.
\State Construct the light-cone overlap graph $G$ and its maximum degree $K$ for $U$.

\If{$K\le1$}
    \State Let $C_1,\ldots,C_c$ be the connected components of $G$.
    \For{$j=1,\ldots,c$}
        \State $a_j\gets
        \mathsf{Brute}(C_j,0_{C_j})$.
    \EndFor
    \State \Return $\min\{1, \max\{0, \prod_{j=1}^c a_j\}\}$.
\EndIf

\State Set $\tau\gets[e^2(2K+1)]^{-(K+1)}$.
\For{$i=1,\ldots,n$}
    \State $P(1_i)\gets\mathsf{Brute}(\{i\},1_i)$.
\EndFor
\If{$\sum_{i=1}^n P(1_i) \ge(K+1)\log(1/\varepsilon)$}
    \State \Return $0$.
\EndIf

\State $\mathcal{B}\gets\{i: P(1_i) > \tau\}$;
       $\mathcal{G}\gets[n]\setminus \mathcal{B}$.
\If{$\mathcal{B}=\emptyset$}
    \State \Return $\min\{1, \max\{0, \mathsf{Good}([n],\varepsilon)\}\}$.
\ElsIf{$\mathcal{G}=\emptyset$}
    \State \Return $\min\{1, \max\{0, \mathsf{Brute}([n],0^n)\}\}$.
\EndIf
\State $\ell\gets |\mathcal{B}| +\lceil\log_2(2/\varepsilon)\rceil$.
\State Enumerate without repetitions
       $\mathcal A\gets
       \{T\subseteq \mathcal{G}:T\text{ is path-connected to }\mathcal{B},\ |T|\le\ell\}$.
\Statex \hspace{\algorithmicindent}
        Include the empty attachment $T=\varnothing$.
\State $a\gets0$.

\ForAll{$T\in\mathcal A$}
    \State $T'\gets[n]\setminus N_G[\mathcal{B}\cup T]$.
    \State $u\gets\mathsf{Brute}(\mathcal{B}\cup T,(0_\mathcal{B},1_T))$.
    \State $v\gets\mathsf{Good}(T',\varepsilon/[2(|\mathcal{A}|+1)])$.
    \State $a\gets a+(-1)^{|T|}uv$
\EndFor

\State \Return $\min\{1, \max\{0, a\}\}$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\subsection{Correctness}
Every call to $\mathsf{Brute}$ returns an exact probability and all arithmetic operations are assumed to be exact as per prior work~\cite{BGM,BGL}.
Thus, there are two possible sources of approximation error: truncating the inclusion-exclusion expansion and approximating the remaining marginals of the good bits with $\mathsf{Good}$.

\begin{proof}[Proof of correctness in
Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm-main}]
After applying the output bit flips, $P(0^n)$ for the modified circuit
is the requested probability $P(x)$ for the original circuit. We
therefore prove the additive-error guarantee for the modified circuit.

If $K\le1$, each connected component $C_j$ has at most two vertices,
and the output vectors on distinct components are jointly independent
by Lemma~\ref{lemma:light-cone-facts}. Every factor $a_j=P(0_{C_j})$ is exact, so their product equals $P(0^n)$ and this branch has zero error.

Now suppose $K\ge2$. The singleton probabilities and their sum
$\sum_iP(1_i)$ are exact. If
$\sum_iP(1_i) \ge(K+1)\log(1/\varepsilon)$, Lemma~\ref{lemma:when_to_output_zero} gives $P(0^n)\le\varepsilon$, so returning zero has error at most $\varepsilon$.

Otherwise, $\sum_iP(1_i)<(K+1)\log(1/\varepsilon)$. The exact definition
$\mathcal B=\{i:P(1_i)>\tau\}$ ensures that every $i\in\mathcal G$ satisfies $P(1_i)\le\tau$. For any $W\subseteq\mathcal G$, Lemma~\ref{lemma:light-cone-facts} makes $G[W]$ a strong dependency graph of maximum degree at most $K$. Its marginals can be computed exactly in $2^{O_{d,k}(|S|)}$ operations by $\mathsf{Brute}$ so all hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:MW} hold.
If $\mathcal B=\emptyset$, the returned value
$\mathsf{Good}([n],\varepsilon)$ estimates $P(0^n)$ directly with error at most $\varepsilon$.
If $\mathcal G=\emptyset$, the returned value
$\mathsf{Brute}([n],0^n)$ equals $P(0^n)$ exactly and this branch has zero error.

It remains to consider the branch where both sets are nonempty.
For each $T$, write
$T'=[n]\setminus N_G[\mathcal B\cup T]$. The exact decomposition is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:correctness-decomposition}
 P(0^n)=
 \sum_{\substack{T\subseteq\mathcal G\\
                  T\text{ path-connected to }\mathcal B}}
 (-1)^{|T|}
 P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)P(0_{T'}).
\end{equation}

To verify this identity, apply inclusion--exclusion to the good
output bits while enforcing zero on $\mathcal B$. For a selected set
$S\subseteq\mathcal G$, let $T$ contain precisely the components
of $G[S]$ that have a neighbor in $\mathcal B$. Then $S\setminus T$
has no edge to $\mathcal B\cup T$, and the corresponding output
vectors are independent. For each fixed $T$, the possible remainders
are exactly all subsets of $T'$. Their inclusion--exclusion sum is
$P(0_{T'})$, proving the identity.

Let $\mathcal A$ contain all possible $T$s of size at most
$\ell=|\mathcal B|+\lceil\log_2(2/\varepsilon)\rceil$, including the empty set after performing the truncation. 
Lemma~\ref{lem:truncating_error} gives \[\left|P(0^n)-\sum_{T\in\mathcal A}(-1)^{|T|}P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T) P(0_{T'})\right|\le\varepsilon/2.\]
For each retained $T \in \mathcal A$, the algorithm computes $P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)$ exactly and
$\mathsf{Good}(T',\eta)$ with $\eta=\varepsilon/[2(|\mathcal A|+1)]$.
Since $0\le P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)\le1$,
\[
 |P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)\mathsf{Good}(T',\eta) -P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)P(0_{T'})|=P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T)|\mathsf{Good}(T',\eta) -P(0_{T'})|\le\eta.
\]
The accumulated sum $a$ therefore satisfies
\[
 \left|a - \sum_{T\in\mathcal A}(-1)^{|T|}P(0_{\mathcal B},1_T) P(0_{T'})\right|\le |\mathcal A|\eta
 =\frac{|\mathcal A|\varepsilon}{2(|\mathcal A|+1)}<\frac\varepsilon2,
 \qquad
 |a-P(0^n)|<\frac\varepsilon2+\frac\varepsilon2
 =\varepsilon.
\]
Projection onto $[0,1]$ cannot increase the error. We now see that every branch has additive approximation error at most $\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Time complexity}
Next, we show that the algorithm has polynomial runtime while deferring the more explicit calculation to the appendix~\ref{app:time}.
\begin{proof}[Proof of the time complexity statement in
Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm-main}]

The dominant contribution to the runtime comes from enumerating over sets $T$ that are path-connected to $\mathcal B$ and, for each such set, computing both the contribution from $\mathcal B \cup T$ by brute force and the contribution from $T'$ using cluster expansion. To bound this runtime, first note that the number of such sets is at most
\[
1+\sum_{s=1}^{\ell} 2^{s+|\mathcal B|}(eK)^s
= O\left(\mathrm{poly}(\varepsilon^{-1})\right),
\]
since $|\mathcal B|+\ell=O(\log \varepsilon^{-1})$. By Eq~\ref{eq:brute-runtime}, the marginal on $\mathcal B\cup T$ can be computed in time that is exponential in $|\mathcal B\cup T| = O(\log \varepsilon^{-1})$ and thus its runtime is polynomial: $O\left(\mathrm{poly}(L_{\mathrm{in}},\varepsilon^{-1})\right)$. Finally the cluster expansion on $T'$ can be computed in time $O\left(\mathrm{poly}(L_{\mathrm{in}},\varepsilon^{-1} )\right))$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:MW}. Putting this together, we have that the total runtime is $O\left(\mathrm{poly}(L_{\mathrm{in}},\varepsilon^{-1}) \right)$.
\end{proof}

