\section{Fine-grained time complexity}
\label{app:time}

Here we keep $d,K\le k^{2d}$ explicit and compute the runtime. 
\begin{proof}[Proof of the time complexity statement in
Theorem~\ref{thm:algorithm-main} keeping $d,K\le k^{2d}$ explicit]
For $K\ge2$, write
\[
 C_K=\frac{K+1}{\tau}
 =(K+1)[e^2(2K+1)]^{K+1}.
\]
Reading the circuit and constructing $G$ take
$\poly(n,L_{\rm in},d,k^d)$ time.
If $K\le1$, there are at most $n$ $\mathsf{Brute}$ calls
on at most two output bits each, followed by at most $n$
multiplications, so this branch has cost
$O(ndk^d\,2^{2k^d+k})$, in addition to preprocessing.
For $K\ge2$, the $n$ $\mathsf{Brute}$ calls for the exact
computation of the singleton probabilities have cost
$O(ndk^d\,2^{k^d+k})$.

On the branch where we cannot simply output zero,
Section~\ref{sec:core} bounds the size of the set of bad bits by
\[
 |\mathcal B|
 <\frac{K+1}{\tau}\log(1/\varepsilon)
 =C_K\log(1/\varepsilon).
\]
The branch that considers the case where all output bits are
good takes time $T_{\mathsf{Good}}([n],\varepsilon)$, with the
explicit bound given below.
In the branch where all output bits are bad, we have
$n=|\mathcal B|$, and the exact light-cone calculation in \cite{BGM} gives
\[
 T_{\mathsf{Brute}}(n)
 =O(dk^d n\,2^{k^d n+k})
 \le O(dk^d n\,2^k)
       (1/\varepsilon)^{O(k^d C_K)}.
\]

Finally, in the branch where the bits are neither all good
nor all bad, set $R=|\mathcal B|+\ell$, where
$\ell=|\mathcal B|+\lceil\log_2(2/\varepsilon)\rceil$.

Then
\[
 R\le(2C_K+3)\log(2/\varepsilon).
\]
By an argument in Section~\ref{sec:core}, the number of $T$s
that we did not truncate, including the empty set, is
\[
 |\mathcal A|
 \le1+\sum_{s=1}^{\ell}2^{s+b-1}(eK)^s
 \le(2/\varepsilon)^{O(C_K\log(K+2))}.
\]
These $T$s can also be enumerated as follows.
For enumeration only, add edges forming a path through all
vertices of $\mathcal B$. The resulting graph has maximum
degree at most $K+2$. Since every connected component of
$G[T]$ is adjacent to $\mathcal B$, the set
$\mathcal B\cup T$ is connected in this enlarged graph.

Fix a vertex in $\mathcal B$. Starting from this vertex,
enumerate all sequences of at most $2(R-1)$ moves along edges
of the enlarged graph, allowing vertices to be revisited.
Every connected set of $r\le R$ vertices containing the
starting vertex can be visited in at most $2(r-1)$ moves
without leaving that set. Thus every admissible
$\mathcal B\cup T$ occurs as the set of vertices visited by
one of these sequences.

For each sequence, let $V_{\mathrm{seq}}$ be its set of visited
vertices. Keep it only if $\mathcal B\subseteq V_{\mathrm{seq}}$
and $T=V_{\mathrm{seq}}\setminus\mathcal B$ satisfies
$|T|\le\ell$ and every connected component of $G[T]$ is adjacent
to $\mathcal B$ in the original graph. Remove duplicate sets
$T$. Since each move has at most $K+2$ choices, the total cost is

\[
 \poly(n,R)\sum_{j=0}^{2(R-1)}(K+2)^j
 \le
 \poly(n,R)
 (2/\varepsilon)^{O(C_K\log(K+2))},
\]
using $R\le(2C_K+3)\log(2/\varepsilon)$.
Note that the added edges are used only for enumeration.
Every probability calculation still uses the original graph $G$.

For each $T$ that was not truncated, the $\mathsf{Brute}$
call acts on $s=|\mathcal B|+|T|\le R$ outputs.

Eq.~\eqref{eq:brute-runtime} tells us it takes time
\[
 T_{\mathsf{Brute}}(s)
 \le O(dk^d R\,2^{k^dR+k})
 \le O(dk^d R\,2^k)
       (2/\varepsilon)^{O(k^d C_K)}.
\]
Computing $T'$ costs polynomial time. The $\mathsf{Good}$
call uses

\[
 \eta=\frac{\varepsilon}{2(M+1)},
 \qquad
 \eta^{-1}=\frac{2(M+1)}{\varepsilon}
 \le(2/\varepsilon)^{O(C_K\log(K+2))}.
\]

Its runtime cost can be made explicit as follows.
The required marginals are supplied by $\mathsf{Brute}$ calls
with $O(dk^d|S|\,2^{k^d|S|+k})$ cost.
At our choice of $\tau$, the logarithmic truncation estimate
of~\cite{mann2025approximate} is at most $ne^{-m}$ at order $m$. Thus
\[
 m=\left\lceil\log\frac{2n}{\eta}\right\rceil
\]
suffices for relative error at most $\eta$.
An implementation of this same logarithmic truncation gives
\[
 T_{\mathsf{Good}}(W,\eta)
 \le
 \poly(n,L_{\rm in},d,k^d,K,m)\,2^k
 \left[2^{k^d+1}(K+1)^2\right]^m
\]
for every $W\subseteq\mathcal G$.

To justify this computational bound, define
\[
 F_S(z)=\sum_{A\subseteq S}(-z)^{|A|}P(1_A),
 \qquad
 h_U(z)=\sum_{S\subseteq U}
       (-1)^{|U|-|S|}\log F_S(z),
\]
where logarithms are formal power series at zero.
Inclusion--exclusion gives
$\log F_W(z)=\sum_{U\subseteq W}h_U(z)$.
Independence implies that $h_U=0$ if $G[U]$ is disconnected.
Moreover, $h_U$ has no term of degree less than $|U|$:
a degree-$j$ term involves a union of at most $j$ vertices,
and the alternating sum cancels it unless that union is $U$.
It therefore suffices to enumerate connected sets $U$ of
size at most $m$.

As in the preceding enumeration argument, walks of length
at most $2(m-1)$ starting at each vertex give at most
$n(K+1)^{2m}$ candidates. Discard visited sets of size
greater than $m$ and remove duplicates.
For each remaining $U$, evaluating all $P(1_A)$ with
$A\subseteq U$ costs at most
\[
 O\!\left(dk^d m\,2^k(1+2^{k^d})^m\right).
\]
Constructing all $F_S$ directly costs
$3^m\poly(m)$ further operations, and computing their formal
logarithms through degree $m$ costs $2^m\poly(m)$ operations.
These costs give the asserted bound on
$T_{\mathsf{Good}}(W,\eta)$.

Since
\[
 m=O\!\left(
       \log n+
       C_K\log(K+2)\log(2/\varepsilon)
      \right),
\]
each $\mathsf{Good}$ call has cost at most
\[
 \begin{split}
 &\poly(n,L_{\rm in},d,k^d,K,m)\,2^k\,
  n^{O(k^d+\log(K+2))}\\
 &\qquad{}\times
 \left(\frac{2}{\varepsilon}\right)^{
   O\left(C_K(k^d+\log(K+2))\log(K+2)\right)}.
 \end{split}
\]
The branch where all output bits are good satisfies this
bound by taking $\eta=\varepsilon$.

Finally, including all the arithmetic costs that are dependent
on $|\mathcal A|$ gives, for $K\ge2$, total cost at most
\[
 \begin{split}
 &\poly(n,L_{\rm in},d,k^d,K)\,2^k\,
  n^{O(k^d+\log(K+2))}\\
 &\qquad{}\times
 \left(\frac{2}{\varepsilon}\right)^{
   O\left(C_K(k^d+\log(K+2))\log(K+2)\right)}.
 \end{split}
\]
For fixed $d,k$, both $K\le k^{2d}$ and $C_K$ are bounded
constants, so every branch has total cost
$\poly_{d,k}(n,L_{\rm in},1/\varepsilon)$.
\end{proof}

