\section{Introduction}

Stabilizer codes -- quantum error correcting codes wherein the codespace is specified as the mutual $+1$ eigenspace of a group of operators -- form a central framework in quantum error correction. They are highly valued for their combination of broad expressive power with a structure that facilitates a wide range of analysis. The earliest and most extensively studied examples are qubit Pauli stabilizer codes \cite{Gottesman_StabilizerCodesQuantum_1997,CalderbankEtAl_QuantumErrorCorrection_97}, specified by an abelian subgroup $\mathcal{S}\subseteq \mathcal{P}_n$ of the $n$-qubit Pauli group. The codespace is the mutual $+1$ eigenspace of all of the stabilizers $s\in\mathcal{S}$. Given a Pauli stabilizer code, one can efficiently compute a number of key properties, such as the code dimension, logical states, and some logical operators \cite{Gottesman_StabilizerCodesQuantum_1997}. It is also straightforward to extend the Pauli stabilizer formalism from qubits to prime-dimensional qudits, with many analogous results following naturally \cite{Knill_NonbinaryUnitaryError_96,Gottesman_StabilizerCodesQuantum_1997,AshikhminKnill_NonbinaryQuantumStabilizer_01,KetkarEtAl_NonbinaryStabilizerCodes_06, AharonovBen-Or_FaultTolerantQuantumComputation_08a,NadellaKlappenecker_StabilizerCodesFrobenius_12}.

The Pauli stabilizer formalism has also become a useful tool for condensed matter theorists, as it provides a unified language capable of describing both quantum error correcting codes and topological quantum matter. When a stabilizer group $\mathcal{S}$ admits a set of generators $\mathcal{G}\subseteq \mathcal{S}$ that are spatially local on a $d$-dimensional lattice, one can construct a \textit{stabilizer Hamiltonian} from these generators by taking the equal-weight negative sum $H=-\sum_{s\in \cal G}s$ whose groundstate subspace is precisely the codespace of $\mathcal{S}$ \cite{Kitaev_FaulttolerantQuantumComputation_03,DennisEtAl_TopologicalQuantumMemory_02}. When a stabilizer Hamiltonian realizes a topologically ordered phase, the corresponding code is called a topological stabilizer code and enjoys topological protection from local errors \cite{Kitaev_FaulttolerantQuantumComputation_03,DennisEtAl_TopologicalQuantumMemory_02,BombinMartin-Delgado_TopologicalQuantumDistillation_06a,KoenigEtAl_QuantumComputationTuraev_10,SucharaEtAl_ConstructionsNoiseThreshold_11,Bombin_IntroductionTopologicalQuantum_13}. Conversely, any frustration-free commuting Pauli Hamiltonian can be interpreted as a Pauli stabilizer code. 

The paradigmatic model at the intersection of quantum error correction and condensed matter theory is the celebrated Kitaev toric code \cite{Kitaev_FaulttolerantQuantumComputation_03}. In this model, one can identify the syndromes caused by Pauli errors on the codespace with anyonic excitations above the vacuum. Knowledge of topological order further enables the identification of logical errors with the transport of anyons around noncontractible cycles of the torus and motivates decoding via matching algorithms which implement pairwise anyon annihilation \cite{DennisEtAl_TopologicalQuantumMemory_02,Brown_ConservationLawsQuantum_22,HiggottGidney_SparseBlossomCorrecting_25}. Topological insights also facilitate the design of new codes. These include surface codes with boundaries \cite{BravyiKitaev_QuantumCodesLattice_98}, punctures \cite{RaussendorfHarrington_FaultTolerantQuantumComputation_07,BrownEtAl_PokingHolesCutting_17}, and twist defects \cite{Bombin_TopologicalOrderTwist_10,HastingsGeller_ReducedSpacetimeTime_15}, which utilize the physics of anyon condensation at gapped boundaries \cite{BeigiEtAl_QuantumDoubleModel_11,KitaevKong_ModelsGappedBoundaries_12,Burnell_AnyonCondensationIts_18} and topological defects \cite{BarkeshliEtAl_TwistDefectsProjective_13,BarkeshliEtAl_SymmetryFractionalizationDefects_19} to encode logical information.

The correspondence between quantum error correction and topological order leads to a natural question of interest to both communities: \textit{What topological phases of matter can be realized by stabilizer Hamiltonians?} A restricted version of this question was answered by Haah in Ref.~\cite{Haah_ClassificationTranslationInvariant_21}. In particular, Haah proved that every translationally invariant Pauli stabilizer Hamiltonian on prime $p$-dimensional qudits lies in the same phase as a decoupled stack of some number of $\mathbb{Z}_p$ toric codes \cite{Haah_ClassificationTranslationInvariant_21}. One can seek natural generalizations of this result by relaxing one or both of the following assumptions:
\renewcommand{\labelenumi}{(\roman{enumi})}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item prime-dimensional qudits,
    \item stabilizers drawn from the Pauli group.
\end{enumerate}
Either of these relaxations lead to models that realize more general topological orders. In particular, the topological orders realizable by Pauli stabilizer models on composite-dimensional qudits --  relaxing (i) while enforcing (ii) -- have been closely studied \cite{EllisonEtAl_PauliStabilizerModels_22}. Ellison et al.~demonstrate in Ref.~\cite{EllisonEtAl_PauliStabilizerModels_22} that models of this kind can realize every two-dimensional abelian topological order which admits a gapped boundary. Yang and Yu further clarify qualitative distinctions between the topological phases realizable by lattice Pauli stabilizer models and those described by continuum TQFTs \cite{YangYu_ClassificationPauliStabilizer_26}.

Over the past decade, there has been substantial progress in the design of fault-tolerant non-Clifford logical operations \cite{Bombin_2DQuantumComputation_18,LaubscherEtAl_UniversalQuantumComputation_19,Brown_FaulttolerantNonCliffordGate_20,ScrubyEtAl_NumericalImplementationJustInTime_22,HuangChen_GeneratingLogicalMagic_25,DavydovaEtAl_UniversalFaultTolerant_25,KobayashiEtAl_CliffordHierarchyStabilizer_25, WarmanSchafer-Nameki_TransversalCliffordHierarchyGates_25,ManjunathEtAl_UniversalQuantumComputation_26,zhu2026nonabelianqldpctqftformalism,williamson2026fastmagicstatepreparation,christos2026nonabelianquantumlowdensityparity}, with an intense renewed interest driven in part by insights from the study of topological quantum matter. A common theme in these constructions is the use of code-switching protocols~\cite{AndersonEtAl_FaultTolerantConversionSteane_14,KubicaBeverland_UniversalTransversalGates_15,Bombin_DimensionalJumpQuantum_16} involving intermediate codes associated with \textit{non-abelian} topological orders, phases of matter which cannot be realized by composite-qudit Pauli stabilizer codes. In particular, the intermediate codes are generically \textit{twisted quantum doubles}, generalizations of the surface code based on finite groups which can host non-abelian anyons \cite{Kitaev_FaulttolerantQuantumComputation_03}. This strategy circumvents two fundamental limitations of quantum computation with surface codes: 
the Eastin-Knill theorem \cite{EastinKnill_RestrictionsTransversalEncoded_09}, which states that no single quantum error correcting code can transversally implement a universal gate set, and the Bravyi-Koenig bound \cite{BravyiKoenig_ClassificationTopologicallyProtected_13}, which states that a 2D Pauli stabilizer code cannot implement topologically-protected gates outside of the Clifford group. 

The quantum double stabilizer models discussed in Refs.~\cite{DavydovaEtAl_UniversalFaultTolerant_25,KobayashiEtAl_CliffordHierarchyStabilizer_25, WarmanSchafer-Nameki_TransversalCliffordHierarchyGates_25,ManjunathEtAl_UniversalQuantumComputation_26} are able to circumvent the Bravyi-Koenig bound because they require non-Pauli gates such as $CZ$, $S$, and $T$, to be included in the stabilizers, which belong to higher levels of the Clifford hierarchy \cite{GottesmanChuang_DemonstratingViabilityUniversal_99}. Accordingly, these codes are sometimes called \textit{Clifford hierarchy stabilizer codes} \cite{KobayashiEtAl_CliffordHierarchyStabilizer_25}. Despite their clear utility, a general framework for constructing and characterizing Clifford hierarchy stabilizer codes is lacking. 

In this work, we construct a general framework for analyzing a large class of Clifford hierarchy stabilizer codes and explore the expressibility of this code family, especially as it pertains to non-abelian topological orders in general, and quantum double models in particular. We refer to the codes we investigate as \xkcd codes, pronounced ``xkcd,'' codes. These stabilizer models on prime-dimensional qudits are constructed from products of Pauli-$X$ operators and diagonal operators drawn from level $k$ of the Clifford hierarchy. This choice ensures that the operator set is a group, even though level $k$ of the Clifford hierarchy is not a group when $k>2$. Furthermore, nearly all of the Clifford stabilizer codes introduced in Refs.~\cite{DavydovaEtAl_UniversalFaultTolerant_25,KobayashiEtAl_CliffordHierarchyStabilizer_25, WarmanSchafer-Nameki_TransversalCliffordHierarchyGates_25,ManjunathEtAl_UniversalQuantumComputation_26} are of this form. We define these codes in Sec.~\ref{sec:XkCd_intro}.

We remark that \xkcd codes are a special case of monomial stabilizer codes~\cite{nest2011monomial}: codes where every stabilizer can be expressed as a matrix with only one nonzero entry per row and column in some basis. Accordingly, \xkcd codes inherit the properties shared by all monomial stabilizer codes, such as a canonical basis for the codespace formed by orbits of a permutation group~\cite{nest2011monomial}. However, \xkcd codes introduce additional structure not guaranteed in an arbitrary monomial stabilizer code---namely, stabilizers belonging to the Clifford hierarchy. This information is closely linked to practical considerations arising in quantum computation, which motivates us to study these codes.

It is natural to consider the class of states which are uniquely stabilized by \xkcd codes. Groundstates of topological codes in general and TQDs in particular may be studied in this framework by placing them on trivial topologies or including logical operators in the stabilizer group, such that the codespace becomes one-dimensional. In Sec.~\ref{sec:XkCd_class} we consider this problem and in Thm.~\ref{thm:unique_state} we prove that the most generic computational-basis expansion of states uniquely stabilized by \xkcd stabilizers is
\begin{equation}
|\psi\rangle = \frac{1}{p^{\ell/2}}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^\ell} e^{2\pi i \varphi(\mathbf{x})}|\mathbf{x}, M\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{c}\rangle,
\end{equation}
where $M$ is a matrix, $\mathbf{c}$ is a vector, and $e^{2\pi i \varphi}$ is a diagonal operator at most at level $k+1$ of the Clifford hierarchy. In other words, if we want phases in our wavefunction ``at level $k+1$,'' then we need an \xkcd code with stabilizers at level $k$. For the case of qubits, $p=2$, this result is consistent with the wavefunctions known to be preparable by either Pauli stabilizers, $k=1$, or XS stabilizers, $k=2$ \cite{HostensEtAl_StabilizerStatesClifford_05a, NiEtAl_NonCommutingStabilizerFormalism_15}.

The questions of what phases arise in the twisted quantum double wavefunction and what operators stabilize it are intimately connected to the universal data that characterizes the anyons in the TQD, as suggested by the methods of Ref.~\cite{Haah_ClassificationTranslationInvariant_21}. There, products of on-site stabilizers forming a Wilson line were used to compute the topological spin of anyons in the topological order, linking the stabilizer formulation to universal data. Furthermore, the cocycle $\omega \in H^3(G, U(1))$ which defines the twisted quantum double ${\cal D}^\omega(G)$ figures prominently in both microscopic realizations and macroscopic descriptions of the phase. 

When $G$ is a finite abelian group, the physical consequences of the twist $\omega$ for the anyon theory are especially clear. Here every 3-cocycle of $G$ may be decomposed into a product of three qualitatively distinct types of contributions, known as type-I, -II, and -III cocycles~\cite{deWildPropitius_TopologicalInteractionsBroken_95}. Of these cocycles Type-III cocycles have the most dramatic effect on the anyon theory. They give rise to non-abelian anyons even though the underlying gauge group $G$ is abelian.

Given the connection between universal data and stabilizer algebra, one may ask a natural question, discussed in Sec.~\ref{sec:realizing_with}:
\begin{question}\label{q:main_intro}
    Given a twisted abelian quantum double topological order ${\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$ with $p$ prime, what is the minimal level $k$ such that there exists an \xkcd codestate realizing the topological order ${\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$?
\end{question}
\noindent This is challenging to answer in full generality and we are not able to fully address it here. Nonetheless we make a conjecture that we substantiate in Sec.~\ref{sec:realizing_with}:
\begin{conjecture}\label{conj:intro_level_k}
   Given a TQD topological order ${\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$, the minimum $k$ such that there exists an \xkcd stabilizer code whose codestate lies in the ${\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$ phase is given by:
   \begin{equation}
       k = \begin{cases}
           2,&\text{if }\omega\text{ is a product of type-III cocycles}\\
           p,&\text{otherwise}
       \end{cases},
   \end{equation}
   where we take $\omega$ to be nontrivial.
\end{conjecture}
\noindent In Sec.~\ref{sec:realizing_with}, we prove the first case by construction and provide supporting evidence for the second case. Our proof demonstrates that twisted quantum doubles $\mathcal{D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$ for which $\omega$ is a product of type-III cocycles are realizable with Clifford stabilizers ($k=2$). Because these topological orders host non-abelian anyons, they cannot be realized with Pauli stabilizers ($k=1$), so $k=2$ is in fact a lower bound. We also highlight a surprising consequence of Conjecture~\ref{conj:intro_level_k}: some qudit TQD models with non-abelian anyons (namely, those for which $\omega$ is a product of type-III cocycles) can be realized with lower-level stabilizers than TQD models with only abelian anyons. We emphasize that this unexpected conclusion only holds for qudits of prime dimension $\geq 3$, as the two cases in Conjecture~\ref{conj:intro_level_k} coincide for $p=2$, i.e. for qubits. 

In Sec.~\ref{sec:gates}, we present an application of our formalism to magic state preparation for logical qudits. We construct a code-switching protocol which includes measurement of \xkcd stabilizers as a key step, generalizing a protocol discussed in Ref.~\cite{DavydovaEtAl_UniversalFaultTolerant_25} to odd prime qudits. Notably, while the qubit protocol probabilistically prepares a magic state, our protocol does so deterministically.

Finally, in Sec.~\ref{sec:discussion} we discuss future directions prompted by our introduction of \xkcd codes.

\section{Review of twisted quantum double models}\label{sec:TQD_review}

In this section, we will review the definition and some key features of twisted quantum double models~\cite{DijkgraafWitten_TopologicalGaugeTheories_90,HuEtAl_TwistedQuantumDouble_13}. While this section helps to motivate the stabilizer set from which \xkcd codes are constructed, readers primarily interested in the quantum error-correcting properties of \xkcd codes (Secs.~\ref{sec:XkCd_intro} and \ref{sec:XkCd_class}) may safely move on to Sec.~\ref{sec:XkCd_intro}. 

The operator set we consider here is naturally suited to the task of realizing twisted quantum double models. Twisted quantum doubles are 2D lattice models whose low-energy physics is described by twisted $G$-gauge theory for a finite group $G$, also known as Dijkgraaf-Witten theory~\cite{DijkgraafWitten_TopologicalGaugeTheories_90}. These models generalize the quantum double based on the group $G$ (which realizes ordinary $G$-gauge theory) by ``twisting'' the $X$-type stabilizers by $U(1)$ phases~\cite{HuEtAl_TwistedQuantumDouble_13}. We will specialize to the case $G=\mathbb{Z}_p^n$, which corresponds to a stack of $n$ toric codes on $p$-dimensional qudits with an additional twist. Given such a stack, the possible twists are classified by the cohomology classes $\omega\in H^3(\mathbb{Z}_p^n,U(1))$~\cite{DijkgraafWitten_TopologicalGaugeTheories_90}. 

The resulting topological order, denoted ${\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$, can be realized by a generalized stabilizer model with qudits valued in $\mathbb{Z}_p^n$ placed on the edges of an arbitrary triangulation of a 2$d$ manifold with branching structure\footnote{A branching structure of a 2D triangulation is a choice of orientation for each edge such that in each plaquette, at least one edge is directed clockwise, and at least one edge is directed counterclockwise. This guarantees that the vertices of a triangle can be ordered, with edges directed from lower-ordered indices to higher ones.}. 
We denote the vertices $v\in V$, edges $e\in E$, and faces $f\in F$. The canonical Hamiltonian takes the form~\cite{HuEtAl_TwistedQuantumDouble_13}
\begin{equation}\label{eq:can_TQD_ham}
    H_{{\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)}=-\sum_{v\in V}\frac{1}{p^n}\sum_{g\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n}A_g^\omega(v)-\sum_{f\in F} B(f), 
\end{equation}
where the $A_g^\omega(v)$ and $B(f)$ are the ``star'' and ``plaquette'' terms, to be defined shortly. 

\begin{figure}

    \input{graphics/TQD_tikz}
    \caption{The geometry associated to the twisted quantum double stabilizers $B(f)$ (left) and $A^\omega_g(v)$ (right) defined in Eqs.~\ref{eq:TQD_Zs} and \ref{eq:TQD_Xs}, for an example triangulation. All colored edges are in the support of the corresponding stabilizer.
    }
    \label{fig:TQD_geometry}
\end{figure}

Borrowing language from lattice gauge theory, the plaquette stabilizers are projectors onto the ``zero-flux'' or ``flat'' subspace, which take the form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:TQD_Zs}
B(f)=\sum_{\{e_i\}\in\mathbb{Z}_p^n}\delta_{e_1+e_2-e_3,0}\ketbra{e_1,e_2,e_3}{e_1,e_2,e_3},
\end{equation}
for all faces $f\in F$, where $e_1,e_2,e_3\in \partial(f)$, the set of edges bordering $f$, and $e_3$ is taken without loss of generality to be the edge oriented against the other two. 
For example, 
\begin{equation}
B(f)\vcenter{\hbox{\input{graphics/B_stab_tikz}}}=\delta_{g+h-k,0}\vcenter{\hbox{\input{graphics/B_stab_tikz}}}.
\end{equation}

The vertex stabilizers, labeled by $g\in\mathbb{Z}_p^n$ and $\omega\in H^3(\mathbb{Z}_p^n,U(1))$, act on all edges bordering the faces which meet at the vertex $v$:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:TQD_Xs}
\begin{split}
    A_g^\omega(v)=\sum_{\{e_i\}\in\mathbb{Z}_p^n}f^\omega_g(e)&\left(\bigotimes_{e_j\in\delta(v)}\ketbra{e_j\pm g}{e_j}\right)\\
    &\qquad\;\times\left(\bigotimes_{e_k\in\mathrm{Lk}(v)}\ketbra{e_k}{e_k}\right),
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where $f^\omega_g(e)\in U(1)$ and the sets\footnote{The notation for $\partial$, $\delta$, and $\mathrm{Lk}$ is standard and originates in the study of homology. In particular, $\partial$ is the boundary operator which maps $d$-dimensional objects to $(d-1)$-dimensional objects and $\delta$ is the coboundary operator which acts in the reverse direction. Furthermore, $\mathrm{Lk}(v)$ denotes the ``link'' of $v$, which for a 2D complex corresponds to the edges and vertices on the perimeter of the faces which meet at $v$.} $\delta(v)$ and $\mathrm{Lk}(v)$ are defined for an example triangulation in Fig.~\ref{fig:TQD_geometry}. The sign appearing in $|e_j\pm g\rangle$ depends on the orientation of the edge $e_j$; the plus sign corresponds to $e_j$ directed out of $v$, and the minus sign to $e_j$ directed into $v$. Schematically, the function $f^\omega_g:\mathbb{Z}_p^x\to U(1)$ is a product over 3-cocycles evaluated on different group elements, where each factor is associated with a face adjacent to $v$. For the triangulation presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:TQD_geometry}, we have
\begin{equation}
    f_g^\omega(e)=\prod_{f\in\delta(v)}\omega|_f,\quad \omega|_f=\omega(g,a,b),
\end{equation}
where $a$ and $b$ are defined for each plaquette as
\begin{equation}
    \vcenter{\hbox{\input{graphics/D_omega_10}}}.
\end{equation}
The algorithm for computing $\omega|_f$ in a given triangulation is detailed in Fig.~\ref{fig:D_omega}.

