We here recall the algebraic relations between the terms appearing in Eq.~\ref{eq:can_TQD_ham}, as first discussed in Ref.~\cite{HuEtAl_TwistedQuantumDouble_13}. Each operator $A_g^\omega(v)$ commutes with every $B(f)$ and the projectors $B(f)$ commute among themselves. Furthermore, $A_g^\omega(v)$ and $A_h^\omega(v')$ for $v\neq v'$ commute on the flat subspace, where $B(f)=1$ for every face $f$. If we additionally take $G$ to be abelian, as in the $\mathcal{D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$ case we primarily discuss, then $A_g^\omega(v)$ and $A_h^\omega(v)$ also commute on the flat subspace.

Notice that the off-diagonal stabilizers defined in Eq.~\ref{eq:TQD_Xs} can be decomposed into a nontrivial multi-site phase $\Lambda_g^\omega$ and an on-site action of $X_g$ on the sites in $\delta(v)$. In other words, we can write 

\begin{equation}
    A^\omega_g(v)=\left(\bigotimes_{e_i\in\delta(v)}X_{\pm g}^{(e_i)}\right)\Lambda_g^\omega,
\end{equation}
where $\Lambda_g^\omega$ is a multi-site diagonal unitary acting on the edges in $\delta(v)\cup \mathrm{Lk}(v)$. Examining the functional form of 3-cocycles over $\mathbb{Z}_p^n$, one can show that $\Lambda^\omega_g$ generically belongs to the Clifford hierarchy. This discussion will be made more precise in Sec.~\ref{sec:realizing_with}.

\section{Introduction to \texorpdfstring{\xkcd}{xkcd} stabilizer models and states}\label{sec:XkCd_intro}

In this section we introduce the $X{\cal{C}}^{(k)}_d$ stabilizer formalism.
The stabilizers we consider are built from products of qudit Pauli $X$ operators and diagonal operators drawn from a fixed level of the Clifford hierarchy. This construction is a natural generalization of the XS~\cite{NiEtAl_NonCommutingStabilizerFormalism_15} and XP~\cite{WebsterEtAl_XPStabiliserFormalism_22} stabilizer formalisms\footnote{XP codes, defined for qubits, are only \xkcd codes when the precision $N$ is a power of 2.}, which allow products of Pauli $X$ operators and on-site diagonal operators. Our construction expands on these works by considering prime-dimensional qudits in addition to qubits and allowing multi-site diagonal gates. (We consider prime-dimensional qudits because the diagonal elements of the Clifford hierarchy are well-understood in this case~\cite{CuiEtAl_DiagonalGatesClifford_17}.) At the same time, our stabilizers are more structured than those of the monomial stabilizer formalism~\cite{nest2011monomial}, which are products of a permutation of computational basis states and an arbitrary diagonal unitary. We begin with a discussion of the diagonal Clifford hierarchy, define the stabilizer models we consider, and conclude with some examples.

For qudits, the Clifford hierarchy \cite{GottesmanChuang_DemonstratingViabilityUniversal_99} is a familiar measure of how ``non-Clifford'' a gate is. If ${\cal P}_n$ is the $n$-qudit Pauli group then the first level of the Clifford hierarchy for $n$ qudits is defined as ${\cal C}^{(1)} = {\cal P}_n$ and the $k$th level for $n$ qudits is defined recursively as 
\begin{equation}
{\cal C}^{(k)} = \{U \in {\rm U}(d^n) \mid U{\cal P}_n U^\dagger \subseteq {\cal C}^{(k-1)}\},
\end{equation}
where $d$ is the qudit dimension. The usual Clifford gates that map Pauli operators to Pauli operators can be found at the second level of the Clifford hierarchy.

A characterization of ${\cal C}^{(k)}$ is not known in full generality, though progress has been made on simpler problems. For \textit{single} qudits a full description was recently given in Ref.~\cite{deSilvaLautsch_CliffordHierarchyOne_25} if the qudit dimension is prime.
Unfortunately, ${\cal C}^{(k)}$ is a group if and only if $k=1$ or $2$ (see App.~\ref{app:useful_results}). To avoid this complication, Ref.~\cite{CuiEtAl_DiagonalGatesClifford_17} focused on the subset of operators in ${\cal C}^{(k)}$ that are \textit{diagonal} in the computational basis, denoted ${\cal C}^{(k)}_d$ with the $d$ subscript signifying diagonal. This subset forms a group under matrix multiplication for any $k$. More specifically it was shown in Ref.~\cite{CuiEtAl_DiagonalGatesClifford_17} that in the $n$-qudit case
\begin{equation}
{\cal C}^{(k),n}_d = \left\langle U_{m,\mathbf{a}} \mid (p-1)(m-1)+{\sum_i} a_i\leq k\right\rangle,
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}\label{eq:U-m-a}
U_{m,\mathbf{a}} = \sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \bb{Z}_p^n}\exp\left(\frac{2\pi i}{p^m}x_1^{a_1}\cdots x_n^{a_n}\right)\ketbra{\mathbf{x}}{\mathbf{x}},
\end{equation}
with $m \in \bb{Z}_{>0}$ and $0\leq a_i\leq p-1$ for all $1\leq i\leq n$, and where the prime $p$ is the qudit dimension. 

In this work we use slightly different notation to that used in Ref.~\cite{CuiEtAl_DiagonalGatesClifford_17}:
\begin{definition}\label{def:cliff_hier_def}
For ease of notation when discussing diagonal gates, we let
\begin{equation}\label{def:Dmf}
D_{m}[f] = \sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n} \exp\left(\frac{2\pi i}{p^m}f(\mathbf{x})\right)\ketbra{\mathbf{x}}{\mathbf{x}},
\end{equation}
where $f\colon \mathbb{Z}_p^n\rightarrow \mathbb{Z}_{p^m}$.
\end{definition}
\noindent From Ref.~\cite{CuiEtAl_DiagonalGatesClifford_17}, we have that
\begin{equation}\label{Dmf_level}
   D_m[f]\in\mathcal{C}_d^{(k)},\quad k=(m-1)(p-1)+\mathrm{deg}(f),
\end{equation}
where $\mathrm{deg}(f)$ is the polynomial degree of $f$, which we define below. 

The phase function $f$ takes in a computational basis state $\mathbf{x}$ and returns a value in $\mathbb{Z}_{p^m}$, which specifies a $p^m$-th root of unity. In order to place $D_m[f]$ in the Clifford hierarchy using Eq.~\ref{Dmf_level}, it is necessary to unambiguously define the degree of an arbitrary function $f:\mathbb{Z}_p^n\to \mathbb{Z}_{p^m}$. 
This is made possible by the fact that every function $f\colon \mathbb{Z}_{p}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}_{p^m}$ can be uniquely expressed in a basis of monomials $x^{k_1}\cdots x^{k_n}$ of total degree less than or equal to $ n(p-1)$~\cite{CuiEtAl_DiagonalGatesClifford_17}:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:poly_decomp}
f(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{\mathbf{k}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n}a_{\mathbf{k}}x_1^{k_1}\cdots x_n^{k_n}.
\end{equation}
Each monomial encodes an independent $||\mathbf{k}||_0$-body gate, where the $\ell_0$ norm gives the number of nonzero entries in $\mathbf{k}$. For instance, if we take $p=2$, then $D_1[x_i]=Z_i$ and $D_1[x_ix_jx_k]=CCZ_{i,j,k}$.
A polynomial, being a sum over monomials as in Eq.~\ref{eq:poly_decomp}, encodes the composition of multiple independent gates.

Because the function $f\colon \mathbb{Z}_{p}^n \rightarrow \mathbb{Z}_{p^m}$ admits the unique polynomial decomposition given in Eq.~\ref{eq:poly_decomp}, there exists a well-defined notion of the degree of $f$. For a function $f$ with monomial coefficients $a_{\mathbf{k}}$,
we define
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{deg}(f) = \mathrm{max}\left\{\sum_{i=1}^{n}k_i \mid \mathbf{k}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n \ \mathrm{s.t.} \ a_{\mathbf{k}}\neq 0\right\}.
\end{equation}
This coincides with the way one would normally define the degree of a multivariate polynomial over $\mathbb{R}^n$, for instance.

To facilitate the comparison of gates with different ``precision'' $m$ we often discuss these gates in a unified way.

\begin{definition}\label{defn:Df}
Let $f\colon \mathbb{Z}_p^n \rightarrow \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ and define
\begin{equation}
D[f] = \sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n}\exp\left(2\pi i f(\mathbf{x})\right)|\mathbf{x}\rangle\langle \mathbf{x}|.
\end{equation}
\end{definition}
\noindent The drawback of this definition is that without information about the degree of the function it may not be immediately obvious how to compute the level of the Clifford hierarchy. However, in App.~\ref{app:useful_results} we see that the finite difference operator always lowers the level of the Clifford hierarchy, and thus we can measure the level of the Clifford hierarchy by how many applications of the finite difference operator it takes to trivialize $f$.

\subsection{The \texorpdfstring{$n$}{n}-qudit Clifford hierarchy}

In this section, we briefly discuss the key definitions of the $n$-qudit Clifford hierarchy. Level $k$ of the $n$-qudit Clifford hierarchy, denoted ${\cal C}^{(k),n}$, is the subset of ${\cal C}^{(k)}$ consisting of operators which have support on $n$ qudits or fewer. By definition, we see that ${\cal C}^{(k),n}\subset {\cal C}^{(k),n+1}$. To further specify the set of level-$k$ Clifford operators with support on \textit{exactly} $n$ qudits, we define ${\cal S}^{k,\Delta n} = {\cal C}^{(k),n}\setminus {\cal C}^{(k),n-1}$. Similarly, the Clifford hierarchy operators which act on $n$ or fewer qudits and are \textit{precisely} level-$k$ are denoted by ${\cal S}^{\Delta k,n} = {\cal C}^{(k),n}\setminus {\cal C}^{(k-1),n}$.

In this work, we focus on the diagonal subgroups of the Clifford hierarchy. We define ${\cal C}^{(k),n}_d\subset {\cal C}^{(k)}_d$ to be the subgroup of level-$k$ diagonal Clifford operators which have support on $n$ qudits or fewer. The set ${\cal S}^{k,\Delta n}_d$ is defined to be ${\cal C}^{(k),n}_d\setminus {\cal C}^{(k),n-1}_d$, i.e.~the set of diagonal Clifford-hierarchy operators at level $k$ or lower which have support on exactly $n$ qudits.

We note that the set ${\cal C}^{(k),n}_d$ forms a group under matrix multiplication. This is because composing elements of ${\cal C}^{(k),n}_d$ can make them act on \textit{fewer} qudits, but not more. However,  ${\cal S}^{k,\Delta n}_d$ is not a group for $n\geq 1$ since it does not contain the identity.

\subsection{Stabilizer setup}

We first define the group of potential stabilizers that we consider. We define $G^{(k),n}$ to be the set generated by products of strings of qudit Pauli-$X$ operators and unitaries drawn from the group $\mathcal{C}^{(k),n}_d$, for some fixed $k$. Our stabilizers are therefore comprised of on-site $X$ operators and multi-qudit diagonal gates. In App.~\ref{app:on_site_clifford} we discuss the case where only on-site stabilizers are allowed. It is not hard to see that every element $g\in G^{(k),n}$ can be written in the form
\begin{equation}\label{eq:main_stabilizer}
g = X(\mathbf{v})D\left[f\right], \ f\colon \mathbb{Z}_p^n\rightarrow \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}, \mathbf{v}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n,
\end{equation}
where $X(\mathbf{v})=\bigotimes _{i=1}^n X_i^{v_i}$ and $D[f]$ is a diagonal $n$-qudit Clifford operator at level $k$. Indeed, commuting any $X(\mathbf{v})$ past a diagonal operator $D[f]$ results in a constant shift in the argument of $f$, which does not change the level at which $D[f]$ sits in the Clifford hierarchy.
This allows an arbitrary product of generators to be brought into the canonical form given in Eq.~\ref{eq:main_stabilizer} and endows $G^{(k),n}$ with the structure of a group. Furthermore, one may immediately verify that $G^{(k),n}\subset {\cal C}^{(k),n}$.

Next, we define stabilizer models constructed from $G^{(k),n}$.
Let $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$ be a subgroup. If there exists some $n$-qudit state such that $h|\psi\rangle = |\psi\rangle$ for every $h\in H$, then we call $H$ an $n$-qudit \xkcd stabilizer group. The space of all states stabilized by $H$ is denoted by ${\cal L}_{H}$ and referred to as the $n$-qudit \xkcd stabilizer code associated with $H$. If there is only one state in ${\cal L}_{H}$ then we call this an $n$-qudit \xkcd stabilizer state.

We motivated the \xkcd stabilizer formalism as a way to classify stabilizer Hamiltonians with ${\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$ topological order. On a torus, the number of ground states is given by the number of anyons in ${\cal D}^\omega(\mathbb{Z}_p^n)$, but if we instead consider the Hamiltonian on a sphere there is a single ground state wavefunction \cite{BarkeshliEtAl_SymmetryFractionalizationDefects_19}. 
First, we restrict our attention to the sphere and consider the case of a unique stabilizer state for simplicity of presentation. This motivates the following definition of the sets of possible stabilizer states:
\begin{definition}\label{defn:stab_sets}
We denote by $\Psi^{(k),n}_G$ the set of states that can be realized as the unique codeword of an \xkcd stabilizer code on $n$ qudits.
\end{definition}

We now give an example of states that require stabilizers at level $k$.

\subsection{Example states that require level \texorpdfstring{$k$}{k}}

A natural class of states to study are those that \textit{require} stabilizers at level $k+1$ of the Clifford hierarchy. These are precisely the states that belong to 
$\Psi^{(k+1),n}_{G}\setminus \Psi^{(k),n}_{G}$. We can use a simple procedure to construct a state in this set.

\begin{example}\label{ex:k_wavefct}
We claim that
\begin{equation}
|\psi\rangle = \frac{1}{p^{n/2}} \sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n} e^{2\pi i \phi(\mathbf{x})}|\mathbf{x}\rangle,
\end{equation}
where $\phi \colon \mathbb{Z}_{p}^n\rightarrow \mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}$ is such that $D[\phi]\in {\cal C}^{(k+1),n}_d$, lies in $\Psi^{(k),n}_G$.

This is easiest to see if we begin with the stabilizer group $H_0 = \langle X(\mathbf{e}_i)\rangle_{i=1,\ldots, n}$, where $\mathbf{e}_i$ is the unit vector in the $i$th direction. Clearly all the generators commute and have order $p$ so $H_0\simeq \mathbb{Z}_p^n$. It is easy to see that they uniquely stabilize the state
\begin{equation}
|\psi_0\rangle = \frac{1}{p^{n/2}}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n}|\mathbf{x}\rangle.
\end{equation}
We now define the group $H = \langle D[\phi]X(\mathbf{e}_i)D[\phi]^\dagger\rangle$. Clearly this group is isomorphic to $H_0$. The group $H$ uniquely stabilizes the state
\begin{equation}
|\psi\rangle = D[\phi]|\psi_0\rangle = \frac{1}{p^{n/2}}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n}e^{2\pi i \phi(\mathbf{x})}|\mathbf{x}\rangle.
\end{equation}

We note that $D[\phi]X(\mathbf{e}_i)D[\phi]^\dagger = X(\mathbf{e}_i)D[\Delta_{\mathbf{e}_i}\phi(\mathbf{x})]$, where $\Delta_{\mathbf{e}_i}\phi(\mathbf{x}) = \phi(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{e}_i)-\phi(\mathbf{x})$. Then by Corr.~\ref{cor:diff_lowers} we know that $D[\Delta_{\mathbf{e}_i}\phi(\mathbf{x})]\in {\cal C}^{(k),n}_d$ for every $\mathbf{e}_i$. Thus this stabilizer lies in $G^{(k),n}$ if $\phi$ is a function such that $D[\phi]\in {\cal C}^{(k+1),n}_d$.
\end{example}

This example suggests that if the phase in the wavefunction is level $k+2$, then the stabilizers cannot be at level $k$. We prove this for a specific choice below.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:state_req_stabilizers}
The state 
\begin{equation}
|\psi\rangle = \frac{1}{p^{n/2}} \sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n} e^{2\pi i x_0^a/p^m}|\mathbf{x}\rangle
\end{equation}
lies in $\Psi^{(k+1),n}_G\setminus \Psi^{(k),n}_G$ for $m\in\mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ and $a\in\{1,\ldots,p-1\}$ chosen so that $(p-1)(m-1)+a = k+2$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof} 
We prove this in App.~\ref{ap:state_req_stabilizers}.
\end{proof}

From this example we can guess that states which \textit{require} stabilizers at level $k$ are precisely those that have ``phases'' in their wavefunction at level $k+1$. In the next section we prove that this is indeed the case.

\section{Classification of \texorpdfstring{\xkcd}{xkcd} stabilizer states}\label{sec:XkCd_class}

The objective of this section is to understand the structural form of \xkcd stabilizer states. This effort culminates in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm:unique_state} at the end of Sec.~\ref{sec:class_theory}, which states that a given wavefunction is an \xkcd stabilizer state if and only if it can be brought into the form presented in Eq.~\ref{eq:state_form}. This result is valuable for state classification, as it can be used to determine if a given wavefunction is an \xkcd stabilizer state, and if so, the minimal value of $k$ such that it is. Furthermore, we present in Sec.~\ref{sec:class_alg} an algorithm which takes in an \xkcd stabilizer subgroup with unique stabilized state and returns the stabilizer state in the canonical form of Eq.~\ref{eq:state_form}.

\subsection{Theory}\label{sec:class_theory}
The main result of this section, Theorem \ref{thm:unique_state}, generalizes the well-known result~\cite{DehaeneDeMoor_CliffordGroupStabilizer_03} (see also Ref.~\cite{VanDenNes_ClassicalSimulationQuantum_10}) that every Pauli stabilizer state $\ket{\psi}$ may be expressed as
\begin{equation}
    \ket{\psi}\propto \sum_{\mathbf{v}\in A}i^{l(\mathbf{v})}(-1)^{q(\mathbf{v})}\ket{\mathbf{v}},
\end{equation}
where $A=\{ M\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{c}\mid\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_2^m\}\subseteq{\mathbb{Z}_2^N}$ is an \textit{affine linear subspace} and $l(\mathbf{v})$ and $q(\mathbf{v})$ are linear and quadratic functions on $\mathbb{Z}_2^n$, respectively. As we will see, the result we present in Eq.~\ref{eq:state_form} takes quite an analogous form. Throughout this section, we will prove intermediate results which are necessary in order to prove Theorem \ref{thm:unique_state}.

We begin by stating a commonly used result on the structure of stabilizer states for any stabilizer group.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:group_comp_wavefct}
Suppose that $|\psi\rangle \in \Psi^{(k),n}_{G}$ with a stabilizer subgroup $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$. Then 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:orbit_psi}
|\psi\rangle \propto \sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle,
\end{equation}
for any $\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n$ such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:x_nonzero}
    \sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
It can be seen directly that $h'|\psi\rangle = |\psi\rangle$ for any $h'\in H$ by relabeling group elements in the sum.
\end{proof}
\noindent This lemma is quite broad and does not yet tell us very much about the structure of \xkcd stabilizer states. However, we are not dealing with an arbitrary stabilizer group, but subgroups $H$ of the \xkcd operator group $G^{(k),n}$. These groups are highly structured, and we will use this structure to impose further constraints on \xkcd stabilizer states.

In the discussion to follow, the \textit{diagonal subgroup} of $H$ -- denoted $D_H$ and comprised of operators diagonal in the computational basis -- will play an important role. We will now precisely define this subgroup and prove that it is normal in $H$.
The diagonal subgroup $D_H\subseteq H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$ is defined as
\begin{equation}
    D_H := H\cap {\cal C}^{(k),n}_d.
\end{equation}
Since an arbitrary \xkcd stabilizer is of the form $X(\mathbf{v})D[f]$, $D_H$ contains all the stabilizers where $\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{0}$. Furthermore, because $D_H$ is comprised of all diagonal matrices in $H$, it is clear that $D_H$ is an abelian subgroup of $H$. Let us now show that $D_H$ is additionally a normal subgroup of $H$, i.e., $hD_H h^{-1}=D_H$ for any $h\in H$. An arbitrary element of $H$ is of the form $h=X(\mathbf{v})D[g]$ and an arbitrary element of $D_H$ is of the form $d=D[f]$, so we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:diag_norm}
    \begin{split}
        h d h^{-1}\ket{\mathbf{x}}&=(X(\mathbf{v})D[g])D[f](D[-g]X(\mathbf{-v}))\ket{\mathbf{x}},\\
        &=D[f(\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{v})]\ket{\mathbf{x}},
    \end{split}
\end{equation}
for any $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{Z}_p^n$. Since $d,h\in H$, we know that $h d h^{-1}\in H$ due to closure of $H$ under multiplication. Furthermore, we see from Eq.~\ref{eq:diag_norm} that $h d h^{-1}$ is diagonal, so we conclude that $h d h^{-1}\in D_H$ for any $h\in H$ and $d\in D_H$. This is precisely the statement that $D_H$ is normal in $H$.

We can further partition $H$ into cosets $h D_H\in H/D_H$, such that every element $h\in H$ belongs to exactly one coset. Let us now consider one of these cosets. If we let $h=X(\mathbf{v})D[g]$, then 
\begin{equation}
    hD_H=\{X(\mathbf{v})D[g+f]\mid D[f]\in D_H\}.
\end{equation}
It will be useful to choose a representative from each coset, such that every element of $H$ may be expressed as a product of a coset representative and a diagonal operator. For every coset $h D_H\in H/D_H$, choose a distinguished element of the coset and call it $r$. The set of all such $r$ is denoted $R_{H/D_H}\subset H$ and called the set of coset representatives, or, more technically, a transversal of $D_H$ in $H$. By a slight abuse of notation, we will write $r\in {H/D_H}$ when we mean $r\in R_{H/D_H}$, and similarly for other quotient groups. Finally, we may uniquely express any element $h\in H$ as $h=r d$ for some coset representative $r\in {H/D_H}$ and some $d\in D_H$. 
\begin{remark}\label{rem:orbit}
Not only can every element $h\in H$ be uniquely written as $h=rd$, but each $r$ may be uniquely identified with some $\mathbf{v}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n$. In other words, two elements of $H$, call them $X(\mathbf{v})D[f]$ and $X(\mathbf{w})D[g]$, belong to the same coset if and only if $\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{w}$. It is clear that they may only be in the same coset if $\mathbf{v}=\mathbf{w}$, as multiplication by diagonal matrices cannot change the argument of $X(\mathbf{v})$. To see that they \textit{must} be in the same coset, notice that if $h=X(\mathbf{v})D[f]$ and $h'=X(\mathbf{v})D[g]$ are both in $H$, then 
\begin{equation}
    h^{-1}h'=(X(\mathbf{v})D[f])^\dagger (X(\mathbf{v})D[g])=D[g-f]\in D_H,
\end{equation}
so 
\begin{equation}
    (X(\mathbf{v})D[f])(D[g-f])=h'\in h D_H.
\end{equation}
\end{remark}

With the coset machinery in place, we are now able to prove a strong constraint on the $\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n$ which satisfy 
$\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$ for a given $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$, as in Lemma~\ref{lem:group_comp_wavefct}. The following lemma will have significant consequences for the structure of \xkcd stabilizer states:
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:wavefct_construction}
Suppose we have an \xkcd stabilizer subgroup 
$H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$ with unique stabilized state. Then a fixed $\mathbf{x}\in\mathbb{Z}_p^n$ will satisfy $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$ if and only if
\begin{equation}
    f(\mathbf{x}) = 0\!\!\!\!\pmod{1}\;\forall f\;\text{such that}\;D[f]\in D_H.
\end{equation}

\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We will first prove the direction assuming that $\mathbf{x}$ is chosen to satisfy $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$. As we saw above, for every $h\in H$ there is a unique $r\in H/D_H$ and $d\in D_H$ such that $h=rd$. This implies that we may separate the sum $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle$ as follows:
\begin{align}
\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle=\sum_{r\in H/D_H}\sum_{d\in D_H}rd|\mathbf{x}\rangle.
\end{align}
Now, choose any diagonal operator $D[f]\in D_H$. Because $D[f]\in \mathcal{C}^{(k)}_d$, it may be written as $D_m[f p^m]$ for some integer $m$. Notice that the subgroup $\braket{D_m[f p^m]}\subseteq D_H$ generated by $D_m[f p^m]$ is normal in $D_H$, so we may further quotient $D_H$ by $\braket{D_m[f p^m]}$. We may then write
\begin{align}
\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle= \sum_{r\in H/D_H}\sum_{d\in D_H/\braket{D[f]}}rd \left(\sum_{k=0}^{p^m-1}D_m[k p^m f(\mathbf{x})]\right)|\mathbf{x}\rangle,
\label{eq:lemma_III_5_wide}
\end{align}
The term in parentheses is equal to zero if $p^m f (\mathbf{x})\neq 0\pmod{p^m}$, otherwise it is equal to $p^m$. Since we have taken the left hand side to be nonzero by assumption, then it must be the case that $p^m f (\mathbf{x}) = 0\pmod{p^m}$, which implies that $f(\mathbf{x}) = 0\pmod{1}$. Further since $D[f]\in D_H$ was arbitrary, we conclude that it must be the case that if $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$, then $f(\mathbf{x}) = 0\pmod{1}$ for \textit{every} $D[f]\in D_H$. 

Now we prove the reverse direction. Suppose that we fix $\mathbf{x}$ such that $f(\mathbf{x})=0\pmod{1}$ for all $D[f]\in D_H$. Then, as above, $d|\mathbf{x}\rangle = |\mathbf{x}\rangle$ for all $d\in D_H$. In particular this means that
\begin{equation}
\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle
=|D_H|\sum_{r\in H/D_H}r|\mathbf{x}\rangle.
\end{equation}
Next, we take the inner product of both sides with $\ket{\mathbf{x}}$, obtaining
\begin{equation}\label{eq:sum_nonzero}
\begin{split}
\bra{\mathbf{x}}\left(\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle\right)
&=|D_H|\sum_{r\in H/D_H}\bra{\mathbf{x}}r|\mathbf{x}\rangle=|D_H|,\\
\end{split}
\end{equation}
where the second equality is due to the fact presented in Rem.~\ref{rem:orbit} that there is a unique correspondence between $r$ and $\mathbf{v}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n$ such that $r = X(\mathbf{v})D[h]$ for some $D[h]$. In particular there is only one $r$ such that $\langle\mathbf{x}|r|\mathbf{x}\rangle\neq 0$, and this is $r=I$. Eq.~\ref{eq:sum_nonzero} tells us that   $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle$ has a nonzero component and is therefore not the zero vector.
\end{proof}

From Lemma~\ref{lem:wavefct_construction}, it immediately follows that \xkcd stabilizer states can be constructed as orbits under the coset representatives $r\in H/D_H$ alone, rather than the full $H$:
\begin{corollary}
Suppose that $|\psi\rangle\in \Psi^{(k),n}_G$ with stabilizer subgroup $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$ and fix $\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n$ such that $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$. Then by Lemma~\ref{lem:wavefct_construction}, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:coset_wvfnct}
    |\psi\rangle = \sqrt{\frac{|D_H|}{|H|}}\sum_{r\in {H/D_H}}r|\mathbf{x}\rangle.
\end{equation}
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
    Using our coset construction, we may rewrite Eq.~\ref{eq:orbit_psi} as
    \begin{equation}
        |\psi\rangle \propto \sum_{r\in {H/D_H}}\sum_{d\in {D_H}}rd|\mathbf{x}\rangle
    \end{equation}
    for an \xkcd stabilizer subgroup $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$. Then by Lem.~\ref{lem:wavefct_construction}, $\ket{\mathbf{x}}$ is a +1 eigenstate of each $d\in D_H$, so we have
    \begin{equation}
        |\psi\rangle \propto \sum_{r\in {H/D_H}}r|\mathbf{x}\rangle.
    \end{equation}
    Finally, we fix normalization $\ket{\psi}\mapsto \mathcal{N}^{-1/2}\ket{\psi}$ by observing that 
    \begin{equation}
    \begin{split}
        \mathcal{N}&=\sum_{r,s\in {H/D_H}}\bra{\mathbf{x}}s^{-1}r|\mathbf{x}\rangle,\\
        &=\sum_{r,s\in {H/D_H}}\delta_{r,s}
=|H|/|D_H|,        
    \end{split}
    \end{equation}
    where the fact that $\bra{\mathbf{x}}s^{-1}r|\mathbf{x}\rangle=\delta_{r,s}$ is true for $r,s\in R_{H/D_H}$ and follows directly from Rem.~\ref{rem:orbit}.
\end{proof}

The simplified form of the wavefunction presented in Eq.~\ref{eq:coset_wvfnct} has an immediate implication for the computational basis states on which the wavefunction can have support. 
We define the \textit{support} $V_\psi$ of $\ket{\psi}\in \Psi^{(k),n}$ to be the subset of $\mathbb{Z}_p^n$ where the wavefunction is nonzero, i.e.
\begin{equation}
V_\psi = \{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n \mid \langle \mathbf{x}|\psi\rangle \neq 0\}\subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p^n.
\end{equation}
Because $I\in H/D_H$, Eq.~\ref{eq:coset_wvfnct} tells us that every $\mathbf{x}$ such that $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$ is in $V_\psi$. It is not obvious, however, that no other computational basis states may appear in $V_\psi$, but this will indeed be the case. As we will prove shortly, it turns out that $V_\psi$ is precisely the set of computational basis states satisfying $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$. 

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Vpsi_descrip}
Suppose that $|\psi\rangle\in \Psi^{(k),n}_G$ with stabilizer subgroup $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$. 
An element $\mathbf{x}\in V_\psi$ if and only if $\sum_{h\in H}h|\mathbf{x}\rangle \neq 0$. In particular, 
this means that
\begin{equation}
V_\psi = \{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^n\mid f(\mathbf{x}) = 0\!\!\!\! \pmod{1}\;\forall D[f]\in D_H\},
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
|\psi\rangle = \sqrt{\frac{|D_H|}{|H|}}\sum_{r\in H/D_H}r|\mathbf{x}\rangle
\end{equation}
for any choice of $\mathbf{x}\in V_\psi$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
We prove this in App.~\ref{ap:Vpsi_descrip}.
\end{proof}

This lemma tells us that \xkcd codes are naturally endowed with a ``flat'' subspace $V_\psi$ which is the mutual $+1$ eigenspace of all the diagonal stabilizers. This structure generalizes the flatness constraint from lattice gauge theory, which requires all plaquette holonomies to be trivial. Just as local Gauss law operators map flat states to one another in a lattice gauge theory, here the off-diagonal stabilizers preserve $V_\psi$, mapping ``flat'' configurations to ``flat'' configurations

Thus far, we have been leveraging properties of the stabilizer subgroup $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$ to prove statements about \xkcd stabilizer states $\ket{\psi}\in\Psi^{(k),n}$. However, the functional form of the wavefunction presented in Eq.~\ref{eq:coset_wvfnct} also imposes a consistency condition on $H$ (if $H$ is to admit a nonvanishing mutual +1 eigenspace):
all of the elements $X(\mathbf{v})D[f]\in H$ must be such that $\mathbf{v}$ is the difference of two elements of $V_\psi$.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:Vpsi_stabsub}
Suppose that $|\psi\rangle\in \Psi^{(k),n}_G$ with stabilizer subgroup $H\subseteq G^{(k),n}$. Let $\mathbf{x}_0 \in V_\psi$. Then $\mathbf{v}\in V_\psi$ if and only if $X(\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{x}_0)D[f]\in H$ for some $D[f]$. In particular this means that if $\mathbf{x}_0\in V_\psi$ and $h\in H$ then there exists a unique $\mathbf{v}\in V_\psi$ and $D[f]$ such that $h = X(\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{x}_0)D[f]$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $h = X(\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{x}_0)D[f]$ be an arbitrary element in $H$. Then we know from Rem.~\ref{rem:orbit} that there is some unique $r\in H/D_H$ such that $r = X(\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{x}_0)D[g]$ for some $g$. But then from Lem.~\ref{lem:Vpsi_descrip} we see that
\begin{align}
\langle \mathbf{v}|\psi\rangle &= \sqrt{\frac{|D_H|}{|H|}}\sum_{r\in H/D_H}\langle \mathbf{v}|r|\mathbf{x}_0\rangle \nonumber \\
&= \sqrt{\frac{|D_H|}{|H|}}D[g(\mathbf{x}_0)]\neq 0.
\end{align}
So then $\mathbf{v}\in V_\psi$.

On the other hand suppose that $\mathbf{v}\in V_\psi$. Then we have that 
\begin{equation}
0 \neq \langle \mathbf{v}|\psi\rangle = \sqrt{\frac{|D_H|}{|H|}}\sum_{r\in H/D_H}\langle \mathbf{v}|r|\mathbf{x}_0\rangle.
\end{equation}
From Rem.~\ref{rem:orbit} we know that for each $r\in H/D_H$ we have $r = X(\mathbf{v}')D[f]$ where each $r$ is labeled by a unique $\mathbf{v}'$. So then by the above we conclude that there must be some $\mathbf{v}' = \mathbf{v} - \mathbf{x}_0$ such that $X(\mathbf{v}')D[f] \in H$.
\end{proof}

The result above also has the immediate consequence that the support is an affine linear subspace.

\begin{corollary}\label{corr:aff_lin_sub}
Suppose that $|\psi\rangle\in\Psi^{(k),n}_G$. Then $V_\psi\subseteq \mathbb{Z}_p^n$ is an affine linear subspace of $\mathbb{Z}_p^n$. In particular if $V_\psi$ has dimension $\ell$ then there exists an $(n-\ell) \times \ell$ matrix $M$, with entries in $\mathbb{Z}_p$, and a vector $\mathbf{c}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^{n-\ell}$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:V_psi}
V_\psi = \{(\mathbf{x}, M\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{c}) \mid \mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^\ell \}
\end{equation}
up to a permutation. Note that the notation $(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b})$ indicates the vertical concatenation of column vectors, i.e. $(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}):=\begin{pmatrix}
    \mathbf{a}\\\mathbf{b}
\end{pmatrix}$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Let $\mathbf{x}_0\in V_\psi$ be a fixed point. Next, choose arbitrary $\mathbf{v},\mathbf{v}'\in V_\psi$. From Lem.~\ref{lem:Vpsi_stabsub} we see this means that there exists some elements of the form $X(\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{x}_0)D[f], X(\mathbf{v}'-\mathbf{x}_0)D[g]\in H$. This implies that $X(\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{v}'-2\mathbf{x}_0)D[h]\in H$ since $H$ is a group. By applying Lem.~\ref{lem:Vpsi_stabsub}, we see that $\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{v}'-\mathbf{x}_0\in V_\psi$. This means that
\begin{equation}
    (\mathbf{v}-\mathbf{x}_0)+(\mathbf{v}'-\mathbf{x}_0)=(\mathbf{v}+\mathbf{v}'-\mathbf{x}_0)-\mathbf{x}_0\in V_\psi-\mathbf{x}_0.
\end{equation}
In other words, $V_\psi - \mathbf{x}_0$ is closed under addition and by repeated addition under scalar multiplication.
This allows us to conclude that $V_\psi - \mathbf{x}_0$ is a linear subspace, which means that $V_\psi$ is an affine linear subspace.

We can now use this fact to write $V_\psi$ in a simplified form. If $V_\psi$ has dimension $\ell$, then $W = V_\psi -\mathbf x_0$ is a linear subspace of $\mathbb Z_p^n$ of dimension $\ell$ and we choose a basis, $w_1, \dots, w_\ell$, for $W$. As the basis vectors are linearly independent, we can always choose $\ell$ indices that determine an invertible $\ell \times \ell$ matrix. We can then permute the indices so these $\ell$ coordinates come first.
Using this coordinate choice, we can freely choose the first $\ell$ components of an element of $W$ as $\mathbf x \in \mathbb Z_p^\ell$, and the remaining $n-\ell$ components are forced to be linearly dependent on $\mathbf x$, or specifically a linear function of $\mathbf x$ since $W$ is $\ell$-dimensional.
Specifically, there exists an $(n-\ell) \times \ell$ matrix $M$ over $\mathbb Z_p$ such that $W = \{(\mathbf x, M\mathbf x) \mid \mathbf x \in \mathbb Z_p^\ell \}$. Since $V_\psi = \mathbf x_0 + W$, we can write $\mathbf x_0 = (\mathbf x_0^{(1)}, \mathbf x_0^{(2)})$ in the same block decomposition to see that $V_\psi = \{(\mathbf x_0^{(1)}+\mathbf x, \mathbf x_0^{(2)} + M\mathbf x) \mid \mathbf x \in \mathbb Z_p^\ell \}$. Here $\mathbf{x}_0^{(1)}$ has dimension $\ell$ and $\mathbf{x}_0^{(2)}$ has dimension $n-\ell$.
Without loss of generality, we can relabel $\mathbf x$ by shifting it by $\mathbf{x}_0^{(1)}$ and define $\mathbf c =  \mathbf x_0^{(2)} - M\mathbf{x}_0^{(1)} \in \mathbb Z_p^{n-\ell}$, which gives us our general form $V_\psi = \{(\mathbf{x}, M\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{c}) \mid \mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^\ell \}$.
\end{proof}

We are now prepared to prove the central theorem of this section. As discussed earlier, this theorem places restrictions on the stabilizer states of \xkcd codes, with a natural connection between the degree of the polynomial appearing as a phase in the wavefunction and the level of the Clifford hierarchy in which the stabilizers are contained. It generalizes the classic result of Ref.~\cite{DehaeneDeMoor_CliffordGroupStabilizer_03} for Pauli stabilizer states.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:unique_state}
A state $|\psi\rangle$ is contained in $\Psi^{(k),n}_G$ if and only if it takes the form 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:state_form_alg}
|\psi\rangle = \frac{1}{p^{\ell/2}}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{Z}_p^\ell} D[\phi]\otimes \mathbbm{1}|\mathbf{x},M\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{c}\rangle
\end{equation}
