\title{\Large The stationarity test: a framework for learning \\ quantum many-body systems from their thermal  states}
\author{Thiago Bergamaschi\thanks{Department of EECS, UC Berkeley, CA, USA. \href{mailto:thiagob@berkeley.edu}{thiagob@berkeley.edu}}
\thanks{Google Quantum AI.}}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}

The task of learning the Hamiltonian interactions governing a quantum system, given samples of its thermal (or `Gibbs') states, is a foundational question at the intersection of quantum learning theory and many-body physics. In this paper, we 
draw connections to the quantum Gibbs sampling literature to introduce a natural learning algorithm we call \textit{the stationarity test}: which simply ``guesses'' the Hamiltonian, and measures the rate-of-change of local observables, under the associated detailed-balanced quantum Markov chain \cite{chen2023efficient}.

We leverage the stationarity test to address the following applications:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item To give the first learning algorithm for the underlying interaction graph, i.e. \textit{structure learning}, of lattice Hamiltonians at all temperatures, given copies of their Gibbs states.


    \item To give the first learning algorithm for the coefficients of a lattice Hamiltonian, given only copies of its \textit{thermal metastable states}, modeled as the ``local minima'' of the free energy. 

    \item In addition, we present a refinement to the recent area law for thermal metastable states \cite{BCV25}, which holds in the thermodynamic limit. 
\end{enumerate}

Our learning algorithms are rigorous, time-efficient, and nearly sample-optimal in system size and accuracy. At a technical level, our arguments are based on new approximate locality and convexity properties for the quantum Fisher information of these Gibbs sampling algorithms.

\end{abstract}

