\section{Introduction}

The Hamiltonian learning problem is a fundamental question at the intersection of learning theory, condensed matter physics, quantum metrology, and device calibration \cite{da_Silva_2011, Shabani11, Wiebe2_2014, Wiebe1_2014, Wang_2017, Bairey_2019}. One important version of this problem is inspired by statistical physics, where nature, or some experimentally motivated black-box, has prepared the equilibrium thermal (or `Gibbs') state of an unknown many-body Hamiltonian. The task is to infer and characterize the underlying Hamiltonian interactions, from measurements on its thermal state.

The classical counterpart to this problem, learning Ising or graphical models, has been studied extensively since the 80's, motivated in part by close connections to machine learning \cite{Ackley1985ALA, Hinton1986LearningAR, bresler2015efficiently, klivans2017learning, hamilton2017information, vuffray2016interaction}. In contrast, only recently has substantial progress been made in the quantum setting \cite{Bairey_2019, anshu2020sample, Haah_2024, RF2024, Fawzi_2024, artymowicz2024efficienthamiltonianlearninggibbs, artymowicz2024certifiedalgorithmsquantumhamiltonian}, culminating in breakthrough time-and-sample efficient algorithms for learning the \textit{parameters} (coefficients) of non-commuting local Hamiltonians, from their Gibbs states at any temperature \cite{bakshi2023learningquantumhamiltonianstemperature, chen2025learning}. However, these results rely on rather sophisticated constructions, such as semi-definite relaxations or complex uniqueness equations for the Gibbs state, and left intriguing directions unstudied   \cite{anshu2023surveycomplexitylearningquantum}. 

In this paper, we present a simple framework for Hamiltonian learning problems by drawing a connection to recent dissipative quantum Gibbs sampling algorithms \cite{Rall2023thermalstate,chen2023quantum,chen2023efficient, ding2024efficient, jiang2024quantum}. At the center of our framework is a primitive we coin the \textit{stationarity test}: which simply guesses the Hamiltonian, and locally checks if the sampled thermal state is (approximately) a fixed point of the Gibbs sampling algorithm \cite{chen2023efficient} associated to the guess. We leverage the stationarity test to recover the existing optimal parameter learning guarantees \cite{chen2025learning}, and to develop the first learning algorithms for two open problems in this literature.

Our first application is to the \textit{structure learning} problem, in which the task is to learn both the connectivity (i.e. interaction graph) and the coefficients of an unknown Hamiltonian.
For example, suppose we receive samples from the Gibbs distribution of an Ising model on a lattice, but without the information of how the sampled bits map to the lattice sites. Can we infer the underlying lattice? Naturally, this is a well-studied problem in the statistical inference literature \cite{bresler2008reconstruction, bento2009graphicalmodelsdifficultlearn, santhanam2012information,
abbeel2012learningfactorgraphspolynomial,
bresler2014structure}, however, has remained a long-standing open question for non-commuting Hamiltonians, outside of very recent work in the high-temperature regime \cite{lewis2026learningstructureopenquantum}. Conceptually, the obstacle is that quantum Gibbs states fundamentally do not satisfy the same exact conditional independence properties that classical ones do \cite{brown2012quantum, 
kuwahara2024clustering,chen2025GibbsMarkov, bakshi2025dobrushinconditionquantummarkov}.

Next we turn our attention to a fundamental issue with the assumptions on the input to Hamiltonian learning problems. Preparing Gibbs states at low-temperatures is computationally intractable in general (at least $\mathsf{NP}$ hard \cite{sly2010computational}), and thus it seems unrealistic to assume that nature, or an empirical black-box, is always preparing a genuine equilibrium state. An alternative, inspired by recent work on slow-mixing classical and quantum Markov chains, is that nature  has instead prepared an approximately stationary, \textit{thermal metastable state} \cite{BCV25, Gamarnik2024SlowMO, rakovszky2024bottlenecksquantumchannelsfinite, liu2024locally, Jayakumar_2026}. 
By arguments akin to gradient descent, these ``local minima'' of free energy are always polynomial-time preparable, but may be statistically far from the Gibbs state, even locally (\cref{rmk:efficiently-preparable}). 
Do samples from these metastable states suffice to recover the Hamiltonian?

Here we give rigorous, time-efficient, and nearly sample-optimal learning algorithms for both of these questions on lattice Hamiltonians, building on the stationarity test. At a technical level, we draw new connections between these learning-theoretic problems and Gibbs sampling algorithms by drawing on modern tools from quantum optimal transport theory \cite{carlen2012analog2wassersteinmetricnoncommutative, carlen2017gradientflowentropyinequalities, Datta2017RelatingRE, Carlen_2019, gao2021riccicurvaturequantumchannels, carlen2024dynamics, BCV25}. Looking forward, we hope this exchange brings forth interesting new interdisciplinary directions in quantum learning theory, as well as new tools for the study of quantum Gibbs samplers; see \cref{section:discussion} for an outlook and for further related work.

\subsection{A framework for Hamiltonian learning problems}
\label{section:main-results}

We dedicate this section to an account of our framework and applications. After some brief setup, we begin in \cref{section:intro-stat-test} with a description of the stationarity test. Then, in \cref{section:structure-learning-intro} we discuss our structure learning algorithm, and in \cref{section:metastab-intro} applications to metastable states.

\paragraph{Setup.} Let $\vH=\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma}h_\gamma\vP_\gamma$ denote an $n$ qubit $\sk-$local Hamiltonian, on a set of non-identity Pauli interactions $\Gamma$ with coefficients $|h_\gamma|\leq 1$. We assume the \textit{interaction graph} defined by $\Gamma$ is of degree $\leq \sd$, and can be embedded on a $\mathsf{D}-$dimensional lattice.  We treat $\sk, \sd, \mathsf{D}$ as fixed constants independent of $n$. At a fixed inverse-temperature $\beta>0$, we denote the Gibbs state of $\vH$ as:
\begin{equation}
    \vrho_{\vH}:=\frac{e^{-\beta\vH}}{\tr[e^{-\beta\vH}]}.
\end{equation}

In the modern quantum Gibbs sampling paradigm, one models the time-evolution of a quantum state $\vsigma$ during the algorithm as a quantum Markov semigroup, generated by a Lindbladian\footnote{The dynamics depends on $\vH, \beta$ and a choice of updates (here, single-site Pauli operators); see \cref{section:prelim}.} \cite{chen2023efficient} which satisfies some form of quasi-locality, and (KMS) detailed-balance, thus fixing $\vrho_{\vH}$:
\begin{equation}
    \frac{\rd}{\rd t}\vsigma=\CL_{\vH}[\vsigma], \quad \text{where} \quad \CL_{\vH}[\vsigma]=\sum_{i\in [n]}\CL_{\vH, i} [\vsigma] \quad \text{ and }\quad \CL_{\vH, i}[\vrho_{\vH}]=0.\label{eq:lind-eq-intro}
\end{equation}
\subsubsection{The stationarity test}
\label{section:intro-stat-test}
The central primitive in our learning algorithms is the following test, which we refer to as the \textit{stationarity test}. Operationally, \cref{def:stat-test} simply ``guesses'' a Hamiltonian $\vG$, and computes the rate-of-change of a local observable $\vO$ under $\CL_{\vG}$ when evaluated on a sampled state $\vsigma$.

\begin{tcolorbox}[
    breakable,
    colback=white,
    colframe=black,
    boxrule=0.5pt,
    arc=2mm,          
    grow sidewards by=2mm, 
    left=8pt,
    right=8pt,
    top=4pt,
    bottom=12pt,      
    before skip=10pt,
    after skip=14pt   
]

\begin{definition}[The stationarity test]\label{def:stat-test}
Let $\vG$ be a candidate Hamiltonian, and $\CL_{\vG}=\sum_i\CL_{\vG, i}$ the Lindbladian dynamics \eqref{eq:lind-eq-intro}. Given a sample \(\vsigma\), an observable $\vO$, and a site $i\in [n]$, we denote:
\begin{equation}
    \mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,\vG;\vsigma]
    :=
    \tr\big[\vsigma\,\CL_{\vG,i}^{\dagger}[\vO]\big].
    \label{eq:intro-stationarity-test}
\end{equation}

\end{definition}
\end{tcolorbox}

When $\vO$ is a local observable, the quasi-locality of $\CL_{\vG, i}$ entails the stationarity test can be evaluated locally, and thus in parallel, which will ensure sample efficiency. We note this is not the first work to suggest (some form of) stationarity as a means to identify thermal states \cite{Bairey_2019, evans2019scalablebayesianhamiltonianlearning, artymowicz2024certifiedalgorithmsquantumhamiltonian}, including practical schemes; we defer a comparison to prior work to \cref{section:discussion}.

\begin{remark} [Completeness]\label{rmk:completeness-intro}
   The main virtue of the stationarity test is a simple \textit{completeness} condition. Namely, if given a sample of the Gibbs state $\vsigma:=\vrho_{\vH}$ and we ``guess'' correctly $\vG=\vH$, then detailed-balance implies $\mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,\vH;\vrho_{\vH}]=0$ for every observable $\vO$ and $i\in [n]$.
\end{remark}

The challenge lies in the converse: if the outcome of the test $\mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,\vG;\vrho_{\vH}]\approx 0$ (for every local observable $\vO$), then the guess Hamiltonian is close to the ground truth $\vG\approx \vH$; and thus we have correctly learned the Hamiltonian. To prove this \textit{soundness} condition, we make use of the following quantitative relationship between the stationarity test and the \textit{quantum Fisher information}.\footnote{The quantum Fisher information of $\vsigma$ relative to $\vrho_{\vG}$ measures the rate of change of the free energy (and thus the relative entropy) of $\vsigma$ when evolved under $\CL_{\vG}$, see \cref{def:relative-fisher-information} for formal definitions.}

\begin{remark}
    [The stationarity test and the Fisher information]\label{rmk:stat-fisher-intro} When input the observable $\vO=\vH-\vG$, the stationarity test measures the \textit{quantum Fisher information} of $\vrho_{\vH}$ relative to $\vrho_{\vG}$:
    \begin{align}
         -\frac{\rd}{\rd t} \mathsf{D}\bigg(e^{t\CL_{\vG, i}}[\vrho_{\vH}]\bigg|\bigg|\vrho_{\vG}\bigg)\bigg|_{t=0} := \FI_{\vG,i}\big(\vrho_{\vH}\big|\big| \vrho_{\vG}\big)= \beta\cdot \mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vH-\vG,\vG;\vrho_{\vH}] 
    \end{align}
\end{remark}

The crux of the framework will be that the Fisher information can ultimately
be bounded \textit{even without knowledge of} $\vH$, simply from quasi-local measurements on samples of $\vrho_{\vH}$, see \cref{section:overview} for further explanation. Next, we discuss the learning algorithms we build out of said test. 

\subsubsection{Structure learning of lattice Hamiltonians}
\label{section:structure-learning-intro}

Our first application is to give the first structure learning algorithm for quantum lattice Hamiltonians, given samples of their Gibbs states, at any temperature.

\begin{figure}[!b]
    \centering

    \caption{A cartoon of the ``neighborhood guessing'' structure learning algorithm. A ground truth 2-local Hamiltonian $\vH$ lies on the edges of an unknown square lattice. The current guess Hamiltonian $\vG$ (edges in blue) is a strict subgraph of $\vH$ (missing edges in dashed black). In each iterative step, we ``guess'' the neighborhood of a qubit, by perturbing $\vG+\vU$ with a small cluster $\vU$ of 2-local terms (orange). The stationarity test then evaluates the rate of change of 2-local observables (purple), evolved under $\CL_{\vG+\vU}$, truncated to a finite radius (gray) around the origin.}
    \label{fig:st-learn}
\end{figure}

\begin{tcolorbox}[
    breakable,
    colback=white,
    colframe=black,
    boxrule=0.5pt,
    arc=2mm,
    grow sidewards by=2mm,  
    left=8pt,
    right=8pt,
    top=5pt,
