\begin{theorem}[Structure learning from quantum Gibbs states, informal]
\label{theorem:structure-learning-intro}
Fix $\eta\in(0,1)$, $\beta>0$. Let $\vH=\sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma_{\vH}}h_\gamma\vP_\gamma$ be an $n$ qubit, lattice Hamiltonian on an unknown set of interactions $\Gamma_{\vH}$. Then, there exists an algorithm which on input at most
\begin{equation}
    \mathsf{N_{samp}}:=
  \frac{e^{\poly(\beta)}}{\beta^2}
  \cdot O\bigg(\frac{\polylog \, \eta^{-1}}{\eta^2} \cdot \log n\bigg),
  \label{eq:structure-complexity-intro}
\end{equation}
samples of $\vrho_{\vH}$, with probability $\geq 0.99$, outputs a Hamiltonian $\vG=\sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma_{\vG}}g_\gamma\vP_\gamma$ satisfying:
\begin{align}
    \textsf{(structure learning)}\quad  \Gamma_{\vG} \subseteq  \Gamma_{\vH}, \qquad  \textsf{(parameter learning)} \quad \max_{\gamma} |g_\gamma-h_\gamma|\leq \eta.
\end{align}
The time complexity of the algorithm is at most $n^{\poly(\beta)} \cdot \poly(\mathsf{N_{samp}})$.
\end{theorem}

\end{tcolorbox}

\begin{remark} [Optimality]
    The sample complexity of the algorithm above is optimal in the system size $n$ and, up to logarithmic factors, in the accuracy $\eta$, matching the lower bound of \cite{Haah_2024}. However, it need not be optimal in $\beta$, Hamiltonian locality parameters, nor time-complexity. 
\end{remark}

We refer the reader to \cref{fig:st-learn} for an description of (a single iterative step of) the algorithm. Roughly speaking, our approach to \cref{theorem:structure-learning-intro} is largely inspired by the parameter learning algorithm of \cite{chen2025learning}, which proceeds by iteratively refining a guess Hamiltonian $\vG^{(1)}, \vG^{(2)}\cdots, \vG^{(r)}$ to increasing accuracy, by performing local perturbations to the Hamiltonian. Here, we replace their iterative step by careful application of the stationarity test; and crucially, maintain the invariant that in each iteration ``more of the structure of $\vH$ is revealed'':
\begin{equation}
    \Gamma_{\vG^{(i)}} \subseteq \Gamma_{\vG^{(i+1)}}\subseteq \cdots \subseteq  \Gamma_{\vH}.
\end{equation}

As a warm-up to our proof approach, we dedicate \cref{section:gibbs-alg} to a introductory derivation of prior parameter learning statements in our framework, recovering the results of \cite{bakshi2023learningquantumhamiltonianstemperature, chen2025learning} where the underlying graph is known. See \cref{section:overview} for further discussion on the proof techniques. 

\subsubsection{Applications to thermal metastable states}
\label{section:metastab-intro}

\textbf{Definitions.} We next turn our attention to thermal metastable states. We operate under the definition introduced in \cite{BCV25}, by casting metastable states as the \textit{approximately stationary states} of the dissipative Gibbs sampling algorithm of \cite{chen2023efficient}:

\begin{definition}[Metastability as approximate stationarity {\cite{BCV25}}]
\label{def:intro-local-metastability}
A state \(\vsigma\) is said to be \(\epsilon\)-locally metastable\footnote{\cite{BCV25}  proposed a ``global'' definition, wherein $\vsigma$ is approximately stationary under the sum $\CL_{\vH}=\sum_i\CL_{\vH, i}$. However, these definitions were shown to be equivalent up to polynomial factors, and to imply a local minima condition for the free energy \cite[Theorem C.2, equation (2.2)]{BCV25}. See \cref{section:discussion} for further discussion.} with respect to a Hamiltonian
\(\vH\) if, for every site \(i\in[n]\),
\begin{equation}
    \left\|\CL_{\vH,i}[\vsigma]\right\|_1\leq\epsilon.
    \label{eq:intro-local-metastability}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

The Gibbs state $\vrho_{\vH}$ is the unique exactly stationary state of $\CL_{\vH}$, however, a metastable state $\vsigma$ may be arbitrarily far from $\vrho_{\vH}$ in statistical distance (e.g. \cref{fig:dw-ising}).  

The virtue in the definition above is that although $\vrho_{\vH}$ may be worst-case hard to prepare \cite{sly2010computational}, a simple ``gradient descent'' argument implies metastable states are always efficiently preparable, even at low temperatures. 

\begin{lemma}
    [Metastable states are efficiently preparable, {\cite[Lemma II.1 and Lemma E.1]{BCV25}}]
    \label{rmk:efficiently-preparable}
   Let $\vsigma_t=e^{t\CL_{\vH}}[\vsigma_0]$ denote the time-evolution of an arbitrary initial state $\vsigma_0$. For time $t$ chosen uniformly at random up to some finite cutoff $\mathsf{T}>0:$
    \begin{align}
        \big\|\BE_{t\sim [0, \mathsf{T}]} \CL_{\vH, i}[\vsigma_t]\big\|_1\leq \poly(n, \mathsf{T}^{-1}), \quad \text{and}\quad \BE_{t\sim [0, \mathsf{T}]} \mathsf{FI}_{\vH, i}(\vsigma_t||\vrho_{\vH})\leq \poly(n, \mathsf{T}^{-1}).
    \end{align}
\end{lemma}

Consequently, by time-averaging, evolving \textit{any} state for $\poly(n)$ time always results in an inverse $\poly(n)$ metastable state \cite{liu2024locally, BH16, Bairey_2019, evans2019scalablebayesianhamiltonianlearning}. We add that the convergence to metastable is expected to be much faster in specific systems \cite{GS23Ising, Bresler2024Metastable, GSS25}.

\paragraph{Parameter learning algorithms for metastable states.} In the language above, we can give a concise re-interpretation to our framework for Hamiltonian learning.

\begin{remark}
    [Metastability and the stationarity test] By Schatten duality, the stationarity test can be interpreted as an algorithmic, local check of the metastability condition:
    \begin{align}
        \|\CL_{\vG,i}[\vsigma]\|_1=\sup_{\|\vO\|=1} |\mathsf{Test}_i[\vO, \vG;\vsigma]|.
    \end{align}
\end{remark}

 \noindent In other words, if a Gibbs state $\vrho_{\vH}$ is sufficiently metastable under a guess $\vG$, then $\vH\approx \vG$! In this context, the question of whether Hamiltonian learning can be performed from samples of thermal metastable states, is informally captured by the following  ``triangle inequality'':

\begin{question}\label{question:triangle}
    If a state $\vsigma$ is $\epsilon_{\vH}$ locally metastable w.r.t. $\vH$, and $\epsilon_{\vG}$ locally metastable w.r.t. $\vG$, then is $\vrho_{\vH}$ locally metastable w.r.t. $\vG$? If so, then $\vH\approx \vG$. See \cref{fig:dw-ising} for an illustration.
\end{question}

 We make this discussion quantitative in \cref{section:metastable-learning}, where we argue that such metastable states suffice to replace exact Gibbs samples used in our parameter learning algorithm of \cref{section:gibbs-alg}.

