\begin{theorem}[Parameter learning from metastable states, informal]
\label{thm:intro-metastable-learning}
Fix $\beta>0, \epsilon\geq 0$ and $\eta>\eta_{\mathsf{thr}}:=\mathsf{z}_\beta\cdot \epsilon$ for a $\beta$ dependent constant $\mathsf{z}_\beta\geq0$. Let $\vH=\sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma}h_\gamma\vP_\gamma$ be an $n$ qubit lattice Hamiltonian on a known set of interactions $\Gamma$, and $\vsigma$ be an $\epsilon-$locally metastable state with respect to $\vH$ (\cref{def:intro-local-metastability}). There exists an algorithm which on input 
\begin{equation}
    \mathsf{N_{samp}}:=
  \frac{e^{\poly(\beta)}}{\beta^2}
  \cdot O\bigg(\frac{\polylog \, \eta^{-1}}{\eta^2} \cdot \log n\bigg),
  \label{eq:metastab-complexity}
\end{equation}
samples of $\vsigma$, with probability $\geq 0.99$, outputs a Hamiltonian $\vG=\sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma}g_\gamma\vP_\gamma$ satisfying $\max_{\gamma\in \Gamma} |g_\gamma-h_\gamma|\leq \eta.$
The time complexity of the algorithm is at most $n\cdot \poly(\mathsf{N_{samp}})$.
\end{theorem}

\end{tcolorbox}

Combining \cref{thm:intro-metastable-learning} and \cref{rmk:efficiently-preparable}, one concludes that metastable states produced by the polynomial-time evolution of the Lindbladian \cite{chen2023efficient}, \textit{on any input state}, suffice to learn the parameters of the underlying Hamiltonian up to inverse-polynomial accuracy. In a sense, this presents a concrete answer to the dilemma posed in the introduction, on whether thermal samples produced efficiently ``by nature'' (modeled as a Gibbs sampler) suffice to learn the Hamiltonian.

\begin{remark}
    [The accuracy plateau $\eta_{\mathsf{thr}}(\epsilon)$] \cref{thm:intro-metastable-learning} holds up to an accuracy threshold. At first glance it seems this is unavoidable (since completeness itself is approximate, \cref{rmk:metastab-complete}), and similarly appears in related classical inquiries \cite{Jayakumar_2026}, but we are unaware of any lower bounds.
\end{remark}

\paragraph{An area law for metastable states in the thermodynamic limit.} The work of \cite{BCV25} proved that such metastable states approximately satisfy an array of structural properties akin to that of the Gibbs state, including a local Markov property \cite{brown2012quantum, kuwahara2024clustering, chen2025GibbsMarkov, bakshi2025dobrushinconditionquantummarkov} and a thermal area law of mutual information\footnote{Defined by $\mathsf{I}(\sA:\overline{\sA})_{\vsigma}:=\mathsf{S}(\vsigma_{\sA})+\mathsf{S}(\vsigma_{\overline{\sA}})-\mathsf{S}(\vsigma)$. The thermal area law for Gibbs states \cite{wolf2008area} stipulates that $\mathsf{I}(\sA:\overline{\sA})_{\vrho}$ scales with the area of the interface between $\sA$ and $\overline{\sA}=[n]\setminus \sA$.} \cite{wolf2008area, SDHS16, KAA21, LS23, DKS25}. The additive error to their approximate area law scaled with the metastability parameter $\epsilon$, exponentially in the subregion size $\sA\subseteq [n]$, and polynomially in the full system size $n$. 

As a simple corollary to the derived quasi-locality properties for the stationarity test, we are able to refine the statement in \cite{BCV25} to remove the system size dependence, thereby providing a meaningful statement in the thermodynamic limit as $n\rightarrow \infty$.

