]

\begin{theorem}[An area law for metastable states]
\label{thm:intro-metastability-area-law}
Fix $\beta\geq 0$. Let \(\sA\subseteq[n]\) be a patch of qubits, $\vH$ a lattice Hamiltonian, and \(\vsigma\) a state satisfying $ \|\CL_{\vH,i}[\vsigma]\|_1\leq\epsilon$ for each $i\in \sA$. Then, the quantum mutual information between the region $\sA$ and its complement in $\vsigma$ satisfies an approximate thermal area law:
\begin{equation}
    \mathsf{I}(\sA:\overline{\sA})_{\vsigma}
    \leq
    2\beta\cdot  \|\partial_{\sA}\vH\|
    +e^{\mu|\sA|}\epsilon^\lambda,
    \label{eq:intro-metastability-area-law}
\end{equation}
where \(\mu,\lambda^{-1}\leq\poly(\beta)\), and $\partial_{\sA}\vH$ are the Hamiltonian terms incident on $\sA$ and $\overline{\sA}$.
\end{theorem}

\end{tcolorbox}

We remark that prior related work has largely focused on deriving area laws for the \textit{exact} steady states of Lindbladian evolution, and generally assumes the dynamics is fast-mixing \cite{BCLSP15, Kastoryano_2013, Firanko_2024}. From these stronger assumptions, they generally are able to derive stronger results. 

\begin{remark}
    Following the counter-example in \cite[Figure 3]{BCV25}, we do not expect the dependence in the additive correction in \eqref{eq:intro-metastability-area-law} to be improved beyond sub-exponential in $|\sA|$. 
\end{remark}

\subsection{Discussion and related work}
\label{section:discussion}

\paragraph{Learning graphical models.} There is a rich literature to the question of learning Ising or graphical models; and we do not attempt to discuss its full history here. Some of the more well-known approaches include neighborhood enumeration or ``Markov blanket'' algorithms \cite{abbeel2012learningfactorgraphspolynomial,bresler2008reconstruction} (which guesses a superset of the neighborhood, and certifies conditional independence using local statistics), greedy ``influence'' algorithms \cite{bresler2015efficiently,hamilton2017information}, the pseudolikelihood method and other relaxations of conditional log-likelihood \cite{Besag75,Ravikumar2010, wu2019sparselogisticregressionlearns}, etc. Notably \cite{bresler2015efficiently, klivans2017learning} devised algorithms which run in quadratic time $\Tilde{O}(n^2)$ (with prefactors dependent on degree $\sd$, for 2-local systems), beating the ``neighborhood guessing'' cost of $O(n^\sd)$. 

We add that our approach based on the stationarity test bears close resemblance to the pseudo-likelihood method when instantiated with classical heat-bath dynamics. In fact, in recent work \cite{Jayakumar_2026} gave learning algorithms based on this method for Ising parameters and structure from (classical) metastable distributions (and without imposing lattice assumptions!); and serves as some conceptual inspiration. However, the relationship at technical level to our approach based on the Fisher information is less clear.

\paragraph{Quantum Hamiltonian learning from thermal states.} \cite{anshu2020sample} gave sample-efficient parameter learning algorithms by proving a strong convexity guarantee for the free energy, albeit the resulting algorithms were not time-efficient. 

\cite{Haah_2024} gave time-efficient and sample-optimal algorithms at high temperatures, based on an algorithmic cluster expansion. \cite{bakshi2023learningquantumhamiltonianstemperature, narayanan2024improvedalgorithmslearningquantum} gave time-and-sample efficient algorithms \textit{at all temperatures} by combining an ingenious polynomial approximation scheme for a certain detailed-balance condition, with an SDP hierarchy. As mentioned, closely related to our work is that of \cite{chen2025learning}, who gave sample-optimal (on lattices), time-efficient algorithms at all temperatures based on the ``accuracy bootstrapping'' idea  (\cref{fig:st-learn}), and a quasi-local identifiability equation resembling a detailed-balance condition. 

The stationarity test proposed here is closely related to proposals by \cite{Bairey_2019, evans2019scalablebayesianhamiltonianlearning} (based on unitary evolution) and  \cite{artymowicz2024efficienthamiltonianlearninggibbs, artymowicz2024certifiedalgorithmsquantumhamiltonian} (Energy-Entropy Balance inequalities \cite{Fawzi_2024}), with impressive numerical performance in certain cases. However, these works do not establish sample-complexity guarantees for non-commuting Hamiltonians.  A series of works, see e.g. \cite{onorati2023efficient, bluhm2026certifyinglearninglocalquantum, Baumgratz_2013, Cramer_2010}, consider learning Gibbs states to weaker error guarantees.

At the broader intersection of learning theory and many-body physics, there is also significant practical and theoretical interest in learning quantum systems directly from \textit{dynamics}, either closed-system (unitary) evolution, see e.g. \cite{Dutkiewicz_2024,  zubida2021optimalshorttimemeasurementshamiltonian, Bakshi_2024, huang2022learning, li2023heisenberglimitedhamiltonianlearninginteracting, Caro_2024}, or open-system (Lindbladian) evolution, see e.g. \cite{möbus2026robuststructurelearningklocal, heightman2026lindbladianlearningneuraldifferential, 
ivashkov2026ansatzfreelearninglindbladiandynamics, romanov2026learningarbitrarylindbladiansquantum, lewis2026learningstructureopenquantum} and references within. We further refer the reader to \cite{anshu2023surveycomplexitylearningquantum} for an excellent survey. 

\paragraph{Quantum Gibbs sampling and metastable Markov chains.} The study of metastable or slow-mixing Markov chains is a centerpiece to the statistical physics literature (see e.g. \cite{Bovier2000MetastabilityAL, Bovier2004MetastabilityIR, Bovier2005MetastabilityIR, BH16}) with theoretical and empirical applications to optimization and inference \cite{SKC94, Baldassi_2016, liu2024locally}.  Inspired by modern developments in quantum Gibbs sampling algorithms \cite{Rall2023thermalstate,chen2023quantum,chen2023efficient, ding2024efficient, jiang2024quantum}, recent work has begun to characterize the structure and applications of quantum metastable states, including bottleneck conditions \cite{Gamarnik2024SlowMO, rakovszky2024bottlenecksquantumchannelsfinite}, connections to error-correction \cite{BGL25}, 
tomography \cite{chen2026efficientshadowtomographythermal, chen2026notestrongquantummarkov, li2026unifiedframeworklocallystable}, etc. 

As mentioned, closely related to our results is a line of work on using Lindbladian evolution to prove new structural properties about Gibbs states, 
including correlation-decay and approximate Markov properties \cite{chen2025GibbsMarkov, bakshi2025dobrushinconditionquantummarkov, BC26, rosaruiz2026staticfeaturesmixingshort} and area laws \cite{BCV25}. In particular, \cite{BCV25} proved an approximate Markov property for metastable states, and reasoned said property rigorously captured a local minima condition for the free energy. Finally, we add that there is a rich literature on the study of fast-mixing quantum Gibbs sampling algorithms, see e.g. \cite{kastoryano2016commuting, capel2021modified, rouze2024efficient, rouze2024optimal, Bardet2023Rapid, DLLZ24, bakshi2025dobrushinconditionquantummarkov, BC26, B26all, basso_quantum_2025} and references within. 

\paragraph{Open questions.} We conclude by highlighting three intriguing directions. First, a natural question is whether one can achieve ``the best of both worlds'' and learn Hamiltonian structure from metastable samples, akin to the classical work of \cite{Jayakumar_2026}. While this task should fit within the framework of this paper, we do not attempt it here for conciseness. More broadly, can we find other applications of quantum metastable states to inference and optimization? See e.g. \cite{liu2024locally} for a discussion on the independent set and community detection problems.

Next, can we decrease the processing runtime of the structure learning algorithm for 2-local systems to $\Tilde{O}(n^2)$, while maintaining sample-optimality, as in  \cite{bresler2015efficiently, klivans2017learning}? It is conceivable that replacing our naive ``neighborhood guessing'' approach (of runtime $n^{\poly(\beta)}$, omitting factors of $\sd$ in the exponent) by convex optimization methods as in \cite{lewis2026learningstructureopenquantum, klivans2017learning} could provide such a route.  

Finally, it remains a remarkable open problem to prove rapid-mixing in 1D non-commuting quantum systems at all temperatures; see e.g. \cite{kastoryano2016commuting, BC26, Bardet_2021} for related efforts. In part, this is since modified log-Sobolev inequalities (which capture a scalar relationship between the Fisher information, and the quantum relative entropy) \cite{temme2010chi, kastoryano2013quantum, Bardet_2021, capel2021modified, Bardet2024, BCL24} are notoriously hard to prove for quantum spin systems. The derivation of the ``approximate convexity property'' for the quantum Fisher information (see equation \eqref{eq:approx-convexity-overview} and \cref{section:fisher-identifiability}) was originally an attempt at this question, and we hope it could inspire further attempts. 

\subsection{Technical overview}
\label{section:overview}

We highlight four key new ideas behind our proof approach, which roughly justify the correctness, time-complexity, the sample-complexity (respectively) of our structure learning algorithm, and then the application to metastable states. We refer the reader to \cref{section:prelim} for relevant preliminaries on weighted inner products, operator Fourier transforms (OFTs), the \cite{chen2023efficient} Lindbladian, and Lieb-Robinson bounds.  

\paragraph{The approximate convexity of the Fisher information.} The starting point to our arguments is an explicit formula for the quantum Fisher information of the \cite{chen2023efficient} Lindbladian derived in \cite{BCV25}, which captures the rate-of-decay of the relative entropy in terms of a carefully weighted norm\footnote{Here, $\rd\mu_{\omega, t, s}$ is a measure decaying in frequency and time, and $s$ is an interpolation parameter, see \cref{def:relative-fisher-information}.} of certain non-commutative spatial gradients of the log-likelihood ratio. Fixing our attention to a single-site Pauli operator $\vA^a$:
\begin{align}
    \FI_{\vG,a}\big(\vrho_{\vH}\big|\big| \vrho_{\vG}\big)= \under{\beta\cdot  \tr[\CL_{\vG, a}[\vrho_{\vH}]\cdot (\vH-\vG)]  }{\mathsf{time-derivative}} = \beta^2\cdot \iiint \under{\|
    [\hat{\vA}_{\vG}^{a}(\omega,t), \vH-\vG]\|_{\vrho_{\vH},s}^{2} }{\textsf{spatial-derivative}} \rd\mu_{\omega, t, s}.\label{eq:overview-fisher-eq}
\end{align}
Indeed, the ``moves'' or updates of the Lindbladian $\CL_{\vG, a}$ are the operator Fourier transforms $\hat{\vA}_{\vG}^{a}(\omega,t)$ of $\vA^a$ (\cref{def:oft}), and thus commutators with $\hat{\vA}_{\vG}^{a}(\omega,t)$ play the role of spatial ``gradients'' (see \cite{carlen2024dynamics} for an excellent survey of this theory of quantum optimal transport).

Ultimately, we would like to relate this Fisher information between Gibbs states (which we can efficiently measure and bound, see \cref{section:fisher-identifiability}) to the distance between $\vH$ and $\vG$. A natural, local way to quantify this distance is captured by commutators $[\vA^a, \vG-\vH]$ with the proper (unfiltered) local Pauli operator $\vA^a$ \cite{anshu2020sample, chen2025learning}; and thus the task reduces to relating the norms of the commutators with $\hat{\vA}_{\vG}^{a}(\omega,t)$ \eqref{eq:overview-fisher-eq}, to that with $\vA^a$. We prove such an \textit{approximate convexity} condition in \cref{lem:uniform-truncated-bare-reconstruction}, of the form:
\begin{align}
  \beta^2\cdot  \| [\vA, \vH-\vG]\|_{\vrho_{\vH}}^{2} \lesssim  \FI_{\vG,a}\big(\vrho_{\vH}\big|\big| \vrho_{\vG}\big) \quad + \quad \text{error},
   \label{eq:approx-convexity-overview}
\end{align}
by building on certain Dirichlet form comparison techniques developed in the quantum fast-mixing literature \cite{BC26, B26all, basso2026spectralgapdaviesgenerator} and methods to regularize OFTs \cite{chen2025GibbsMarkov, chen2025learning}.\footnote{To the expert reader, we remark that this step is a comparison between the Fisher information of the \cite{chen2023efficient} Lindbladian, and the Dirichlet form of the log-likelihood under the `pseudo-Lindbladian' generator of \cite{BC26}.} The above discussion justifies the intuition that if the stationarity test $\approx 0$, then $\vG\approx \vH$.

\paragraph{Lipschitz continuity properties for the \cite{chen2023efficient} Lindbladian.} We next sketch a means to iteratively refine the guess Hamiltonian to increasing accuracy $\eta_1, \eta_2, \cdots, \eta_r=2^{-r}$ (refer back to \cref{fig:st-learn}). 

Following \cite{chen2025learning}, in each iteration the guess $\vG^{(1)}, \vG^{(2)}, \cdots, \vG^{(r)}$ is exhaustively locally perturbed $\vG^{(r)}+ \eta_r \vU$, and (roughly speaking) we pick the candidate that minimizes the stationarity test on all local observables.  For simplicity, in the parameter learning setting of \cref{section:gibbs-alg}, assume for now that $\vU$ lies in some finite radius $\ell$ around a qubit $i\in [n]$ in the lattice. 

By design, one such guess $\vG^{(r)}+ \eta_r  \vU^*$ will be closest to $\vH$ within the radius $\ell$, and we require a means to analyze the performance of the stationarity test under said perturbation. The following Lipschitz continuity property for the \cite{chen2023efficient} Lindbladian answers this question (\cref{lemma:lipschitz}).
 \begin{align}
        \|\CL^\dagger_{\vH, a}-\CL^\dagger_{\vG, a}\|_{\infty-\infty} \lesssim  \sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma} |g_\gamma-h_\gamma|\cdot e^{-\Omega(\mathsf{dist}(a, \gamma))} \label{eq:lipschitz-overview}
    \end{align}
Crucially, if $\vH$ and $\vG^{(r)}+ \eta_r  \vU^*$ agree within the radius $\ell$ (i.e. we guessed correctly, locally) and $\vH\approx \vG^{(r)}$ outside of $\ell$ to some coarse error $\eta_r$, then the error\footnote{The convergence of \eqref{eq:lipschitz-overview} only holds for lattice Hamiltonians, which is why we only enumerate over perturbations $\vG^{(r)}+ \eta_r  \vU$ which preserve the property that the interaction graph admits polynomial growth. Furthermore, a thresholding step implies we only accept guesses $\vG^{(r)}$ which are a subgraph of $\vH$.} of replacing $\vG$ with $\vH$ in the stationarity test decays with both $\eta_r$ and $\ell$! This will enable us to choose the enumeration radius $\ell\sim \poly(\beta)$ to be independent of the target accuracy, which is the key step to ensure time-efficiency. 

\begin{remark}
    By combining \eqref{eq:lipschitz-overview} and \eqref{eq:approx-convexity-overview}, we observe (informally) that we have learned the Hamiltonian if and only if $\vrho_{\vH}$ is metastable w.r.t $\vG$: 
    \begin{equation}
     \vG\approx \vH \quad \iff \quad \CL_{\vG}[\vrho_{\vH}]\approx \CL_{\vH}[\vrho_{\vH}] = 0
    \end{equation}
\end{remark}

\paragraph{A sample-optimal neighborhood guessing algorithm.} It follows from Lieb-Robinson bounds that to evaluate the stationarity test on some local observable $\vO$, to error $\eta$, it suffices to truncate the guess Hamiltonian $\vG$ to some radius $\sR\sim \log \eta^{-1}$ around the site $i\in [n]$. Naively, if one were to perform state tomography to compute such quasi-local expectations $\CL_{\vG, i}^\dagger[\vO]$, we would require a number of samples exponential in the number of qubits in the ball \cite{kuwahara2020clustering, huang2020predicting, aaronson2018shadowtomographyquantumstates}, resulting in a quasi-polynomial $\sim 2^{\polylog \eta^{-1}}$ sample-complexity (factors of success probability omitted). 

At least in the parameter learning setting of \cref{section:gibbs-alg}, there is a simple solution to this issue. We can simply run short-time Lindbladian dynamics (defined by the truncation $\vG_{\sR}$) in parallel, across non-overlapping subsets of radius $\sR$, to get optimal sample complexity $\sim \eta^{-2} \polylog \eta^{-1}$ using a median-of-means estimator (see \cref{section:H-complexity}). Unfortunately, by itself this strategy does not give optimal results in the structure learning setting. This is since we need to enumerate over $n^{\poly(\ell, \sd, \sk)}$ possible choices of each neighborhood guess, resulting in a total of $\poly(n, \eta^{-1})$ samples. 

To get optimal sample complexity for the structure learning problem, we instead combine the two ideas above by revisiting the continuity property for the \cite{chen2023efficient} Lindbladian. Let $\mathsf{L}\gg \ell$ be a (constant) length-scale, and for any perturbation $\eta\vU$ let $\vG_\mathsf{L}, (\vG+\eta\vU)_\mathsf{L}$ denote truncations\footnote{As a technical point, this latter truncation is relative to the interaction graph of the perturbed Hamiltonian.} to radius $\mathsf{L}$. We approximate the stationarity test under the perturbation $\vG+\eta\vU$ with two terms:
\begin{align}
    \mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,\vG+\eta\vU;\vrho_{\vH}] \approx \under{\mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,\vG_{\sR};\vrho_{\vH}]}{\textsf{a ``baseline'' estimate}} +  \under{\mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,(\vG+\eta\vU)_\mathsf{L};\vrho_{\vH}] - \mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,\vG_{\mathsf{L}};\vrho_{\vH}]}{\textsf{a correction at radius } \mathsf{L}, \textsf{ to error decaying with $\eta$ and $\mathsf{L}$}}\label{eq:intro-baseline+corr}
\end{align}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item a ``baseline'' estimate under the current guess $\vG$, by truncating the Lindbladian to radius $\sR\sim \log \eta^{-1}$, and measuring the rate-of-change of observables by short-time evolution (similar to the parameter learning setting).
    \item a perturbation-dependent correction, computed using Pauli shadow tomography \cite{huang2020predicting} on all constant-sized RDMs (determined by the radius $\mathsf{L}\sim \poly(\beta)\cdot \ell$) simultaneously.
\end{enumerate}

We refer the reader to \cref{fig:st-learn,fig:optimal-sample} for informal illustrations, and \cref{lemma:parallel-stat-meas} for further details.

