\paragraph{Approximate detailed-balance and generalizations to metastable states.} Conceptually, there are two central challenges to generalizing our approach to parameter learning to samples from a metastable state $\vsigma$ (w.r.t. $\vH$). First, $\vsigma$ is not necessarily a Gibbs state, and thereby the log-likelihood $\log \vsigma$ need not be local -- in particular, we cannot efficiently measure the associated Fisher information $\FI_{\vG,a}\big(\vsigma\big|\big| \vrho_{\vG}\big)$. What is more, even if we could compute it, metastable states only satisfy a certain rather rigid form of \textit{approximate detailed-balance} (see \cref{def:metastable-adb}, from \cite{BCV25}); and thus it is not clear that the convexity arguments for the Fisher information apply. 

Accordingly, we are forced to slightly re-route the proof of correctness, and instead directly prove convexity of the stationarity test $\mathsf{Test}_i[\vH-\vG, \vG;\vsigma]$ when measured on metastable states. Crucially, we leverage said approximate detailed balance condition to prove an analog of \eqref{eq:approx-convexity-overview}, in that the time derivative of $\vH-\vG$ when $\vsigma$ is evolved under $\CL_{\vG, a}$ is approximately a gradient-square:
\begin{align}
   \mathsf{Test}_i[\vH-\vG, \vG;\vsigma] = \tr[\CL_{\vG, a}[\vsigma]\cdot (\vH-\vG)]  \approx \beta\cdot \iiint \|
    [\hat{\vA}_{\vG}^{a}(\omega,t), \vH-\vG]\|_{\vsigma,s}^{2} \rd\mu_{\omega, t, s},
\end{align}
now weighted under $\vsigma$. We note that this step (\cref{lem:metastable-differentiated-entropy-gradient}) is quite technically involved, but ultimately relies on combining tools from the study of Markov properties in metastable states \cite[Section D]{BCV25} with our new Lipschitz continuity properties  \eqref{eq:lipschitz-overview} and \eqref{eq:intro-baseline+corr} for the \cite{chen2023efficient} Lindbladian. We refer the reader to the correctness argument in \cref{section:metastab-correct} for further details. 

\section*{Organization}

In \cref{section:prelim}, we present the relevant preliminaries. In \cref{section:fisher-identifiability}, we present the connection between the stationarity test and the quantum Fisher information. As a warm-up, in \cref{section:gibbs-alg} we give a parameter learning algorithm from Gibbs states. In \cref{section:structure-learning} we give our structure learning algorithm from Gibbs states (\cref{theorem:structure-learning-intro}), and \cref{section:metastable-learning} our parameter learning algorithm from metastable states (\cref{thm:intro-metastable-learning}). In \cref{section:locality} we present the relevant locality estimates for \cite{chen2023efficient}, and in \cref{section:area-law} the refinements to the area law (\cref{thm:intro-metastability-area-law}).

