\section{Learning Hamiltonian parameters from their metastable states}
\label{section:metastable-learning}

In this section, we prove that the parameter learning algorithm devised in \cref{section:gibbs-alg} succeeds even when input samples from metastable states (as opposed to the Gibbs state). We operate under the local formulation of metastability presented in \cref{def:intro-local-metastability}, and restated here.

\begin{definition}[Metastability as approximate stationarity {\cite{BCV25}}]
\label{def:local-metastability}
A state \(\vsigma\) is said to be \(\epsilon\)-locally metastable with respect to a Hamiltonian
\(\vH\) if, for every site \(i\in[n]\),
\begin{equation}
    \left\|\CL_{\vH,i}[\vsigma]\right\|_1\leq\epsilon.
    \label{eq:local-metastability}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}\label{rmk:metastab-complete}
\cref{def:local-metastability} entails an approximate version of the completeness condition for the stationarity test. For any observable $\vO$,
    \begin{equation}
        |\mathsf{Test}_{i}[\vO,\vH;\sig]| \leq \epsilon\cdot \|\vO\|. \tag{Completeness}
    \end{equation}
\end{remark}

In other words, the ground truth Hamiltonian $\vH$ approximately passes the stationarity test. The following \cref{thm:end-to-end-metastable} is the main result of this section, which learns the coefficients of the Hamiltonian up to an accuracy threshold ``plateau'' which depends on $\epsilon$. As before, we consider $(\sd, \sD, \scgr)$, $\sk$ local qubit Hamiltonians and treat said parameters as fixed constants, in order to study the asymptotics in $n, \eta, \beta, \delta$.

\begin{theorem}[Learning from metastable states]
\label{thm:end-to-end-metastable} 
    Fix $\beta\in \BR^+, \epsilon\in (0, 1)$, an $n$ site $(\sd, \sD, \scgr_{\sgr})$ Hamiltonian $\vH:=\sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma} h_\gamma \vP_\gamma$  on a fixed set of interactions $\Gamma$ with $|h_\gamma|\leq 1$. Let $\vsigma$ be an $\epsilon-$locally metastable state w.r.t. $\vH$. Fix $\delta\in (0, 1)$ and $\eta >\eta_{\mathsf{thr}}:=\scgr_\beta\cdot \epsilon$ where $\scgr_\beta\leq \beta^{-1}\cdot e^{\poly(\beta)}$. 

    Then, with probability $\geq 1-\delta$, the learning algorithm described in \cref{section:gibbs-alg} returns a Hamiltonian $\vG=\sum_{\gamma\in \Gamma} g_\gamma \vP_\gamma$ satisfying $\max_{\gamma\in \Gamma}|h_\gamma-g_\gamma|\leq \eta$ using at most 
    \begin{equation}
      \mathsf{N_{samp}}:= \frac{ e^{\poly(\beta)}}{\beta^2}\cdot O\bigg(\eta^{-2}\log \frac{n}{\delta\eta}\cdot \log^{\sD}\frac{1}{\eta}\bigg)
    \end{equation}
    copies of $\vsigma$ and $\poly(n,  \mathsf{N_{samp}})$ quantum and classical processing time. 
\end{theorem}

We use the exact same algorithm as described in \cref{alg:gibbs-learner,alg:one-round}; however, with minor modifications to the choice of parameters as discussed in \cref{section:metastab-correct} which do not change the asymptotic sample or time complexity. As we discuss shortly, the correctness argument (\cref{lemma:metastab-correctness}) is based on revisiting the approximate convexity property for the quantum Fisher information, and deriving an analogous statement directly for the stationarity test (evaluated on the metastable state). The resulting error will depend on the extent with which $\vsigma$ violates a certain detailed-balance condition \cite{BCV25}, which we introduce in the sequence (\cref{section:adb}). Subsequently, we argue the correctness of our iterative algorithm in \cref{section:metastab-correct}, and conclude with the sample and time complexity in \cref{section:metastab-sample-and-time}

\subsection{The approximate detailed-balance condition}
\label{section:adb}

We dedicate this section to collecting the relevant preliminaries on metastable states, later required to prove correctness. We will make extensive use of the following definition \cite{BCV25} of a robust version of KMS detailed-balance condition, and here we derive several additional implications. Throughout this subsection we recall that the Gaussian energy filter width is fixed to
\(\sigma=\beta^{-1}\).

\begin{definition}[Approximate detailed balance
{\cite[Definition~C.1]{BCV25}}]
\label{def:metastable-adb}
For a full-rank state \(\vsigma\), a Hamiltonian \(\vG\), and a
Hermitian jump \(\vA\), define
\begin{equation}
 \ADB_{\vG,\vA}[\vsigma]
 :=\int_{\BR}\!\rd\omega\int_{\BR}\!\rd t\,
 \gamma_{\mathsf M}(\omega)g(t)
 \left\|
  \widehat{\vA}_{\vG}(\omega,t)\sqrt{\vsigma}
  -\sqrt{\vsigma}\,\vrho_{\vG}^{-1/2}
   \widehat{\vA}_{\vG}(\omega,t)\vrho_{\vG}^{1/2}
 \right\|_2^2 .
 \label{eq:metastable-adb-definition}
\end{equation}
\end{definition}

 A central innovation in \cite{BCV25} was to relate this condition to the gradients of the log likelihood.

\begin{lemma}[ADB and entropy gradients
{\cite[Lemma~C.2]{BCV25}}]
\label{lem:metastable-adb-entropy-gradient}
In the context of \cref{def:metastable-adb}:
\begin{align}
 \ADB_{\vG,\vA}[\vsigma]
 &=\int_{-1/2}^{1/2}\!\rd s
   \int_{\BR}\!\rd\omega\int_{\BR}\!\rd t\,
   h_s(\omega)g_s^{\ADB}(t)
   \left\|[\widehat{\vA}_{\vG}(\omega,t),\log\vsigma-\log\vrho_{\vG}]
   \right\|_{\vsigma,s}^{2},
 \label{eq:metastable-adb-entropy-gradient}\\
 g_s^{\ADB}(t)
 &:=\frac12\int_{-(1/2-|s|)}^{1/2-|s|}g_{s'}(t)\,\rd s'
 =\frac{1}{2\pi\beta}
 \log\!\left(
 \frac{\cosh(2\pi t/\beta)+\cos(\pi s)}
      {\cosh(2\pi t/\beta)-\cos(\pi s)}
 \right),
 \label{eq:metastable-adb-kernel}
\end{align}
where \(g_s, h_s\) are the time and frequency filter functions from the Fisher information
\eqref{eq:gs-explicit}.
\end{lemma}

Under this formulation, the approximate detailed balance condition or `error' can be related to the Fisher information:

\begin{lemma}[ADB and the Fisher Information
{\cite[Theorem~C.1]{BCV25}}]
\label{lem:metastable-FI-to-ADB}
In the context of \cref{def:metastable-adb}, let $\vA^a:=\vA$ satisfy $\|\vA^a\|\leq 1$. Then, there exists a universal constant $c>0$ s.t. 
\begin{equation}
  \ADB_{\vG,\vA^a}[\vsigma]
  \leq
c\cdot  \FI_{\vG,a}(\vsigma\Vert\vrho_{\vG})
  \left(
    1+
    \log_+
    \frac{c\cdot \|\log \vsigma - \log \vrho_{\vG}\|^2}
         {\FI_{\vG,a}(\vsigma\Vert\vrho_{\vG})}
  \right),
  \label{eq:metastable-FI-to-ADB}
\end{equation}
where \(\log_+x=\max\{0,\log x\}\), and the right-hand side is zero
when the Fisher information vanishes.
\end{lemma}

We dedicate the remainder of this subsection to describing two key ideas. First, in \cref{section:multi-to-single-adb} we describe how the approximate detailed-balance condition under single-site updates can be compared to the same condition under few-site (e.g. 2-site) updates, up to additive corrections. We remark on the passing resemblance to the Dirichlet form comparison inequalities in \cite{chen2025GibbsMarkov, BC26, BCV25, B26all}. Then, in \cref{section:kms-to-F} we describe how to relate the KMS norm of local observables, weighted under the metastable state, to the associated Frobenius norm, using the ADB condition. This is in analogue to the ``faithfulness" condition for Gibbs states described in \cref{lemma:faithfulness}. 

\subsubsection{Multi-site to single-site approximate detailed balance}
\label{section:multi-to-single-adb}

The next ingredient we require are two brief lemmas on how to relate multi-site variants of the approximate detailed-balance condition. Namely, suppose $\vsigma$ is approximately detailed-balanced, w.r.t single-site jump operators with certain error. Does this entail $\vsigma$ is also approximately detailed-balanced under slightly larger jump operators?

\begin{lemma}[Multi-site to single-site ADB]
\label{lem:metastable-multisite-to-single-site-adb}
Let $\beta\sd\geq 1.$ Let $\vG$ be a local Hamiltonian on a bounded, degree $\sd$ graph, $\vsigma$ a full rank state, and $\vP = \otimes_i^{|\sR|}\vA_i$ a Pauli operator supported on a region $\sR$ of qubits. Then, there exists a constant $c\leq e^{\poly(\beta\sd)}$ s.t. for every $\Omega\geq 1$,
\begin{equation}
\ADB_{\vG,\vP}[\vsigma]
 \leq c^{|\sR|}\cdot \bigg(e^{4\beta \Omega}\max_{1\leq i\leq |\sR|} \ADB_{\vG,\vA_i}[\vsigma]
      +e^{-\Omega/8\sd}\bigg).
 \label{eq:differentiated-gradient-multisite-adb}
\end{equation}

\end{lemma}

